1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,440

2
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:06,140
Hi, everyone.

3
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:07,498
Let's get started.

4
00:00:07,498 --> 00:00:09,040
In the last couple
of lectures, we've

5
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:12,720
been talking about
training RL and imitation

6
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:16,120
learning systems that can
do multiple different tasks.

7
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:20,625
And we first looked at this from
the lens of multi-task imitation

8
00:00:20,625 --> 00:00:22,000
and reinforcement
learning, where

9
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,420
you condition a policy or a
Q-function not just on the state

10
00:00:26,420 --> 00:00:29,320
but also on some sort of
descriptor of the task,

11
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:30,865
or where there
are also scenarios

12
00:00:30,865 --> 00:00:33,240
in the goal condition setting
where that descriptor could

13
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:36,160
actually be a state
itself, like a goal state

14
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:37,895
that you're trying to reach.

15
00:00:37,895 --> 00:00:39,520
And then more recently,
we were talking

16
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:43,000
about meta-reinforcement
learning, where you could think

17
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,080
of that instead of conditioning
on a description of the task,

18
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:48,160
you want to do-- you
actually need to figure out

19
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:51,600
from a few examples and a
little bit of experience

20
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:54,240
with rewards, what the task is.

21
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:58,440
And you're trying to train
this few-shot policy that

22
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:01,770
can adapt with a small
amount of experience.

23
00:01:01,770 --> 00:01:04,530
Now, today, we'll
be kind of building

24
00:01:04,530 --> 00:01:08,530
on some of these concepts
and trying to not just train

25
00:01:08,530 --> 00:01:11,730
a policy to do multiple tasks
but to actually string together

26
00:01:11,730 --> 00:01:18,210
behaviors for multiple tasks
into a longer horizon task.

27
00:01:18,210 --> 00:01:21,690
So this is where we're
going to be talking

28
00:01:21,690 --> 00:01:24,450
about where hierarchy might
be helpful for solving

29
00:01:24,450 --> 00:01:25,910
long-horizon tasks.

30
00:01:25,910 --> 00:01:27,410
And then we'll be
talking about some

31
00:01:27,410 --> 00:01:30,570
of the key design choices
behind hierarchical imitation

32
00:01:30,570 --> 00:01:32,410
and reinforcement
learning methods,

33
00:01:32,410 --> 00:01:35,330
including how to represent
skills and goals, when

34
00:01:35,330 --> 00:01:39,390
to move on from one
goal or task to another,

35
00:01:39,390 --> 00:01:41,850
and the supervision for
the different levels

36
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:44,085
of the hierarchy.

37
00:01:44,085 --> 00:01:46,210
And then lastly, we'll go
through a couple examples

38
00:01:46,210 --> 00:01:48,850
of what these systems could
actually look like because there

39
00:01:48,850 --> 00:01:50,650
are a variety of
different ways that you

40
00:01:50,650 --> 00:01:53,930
could design these sort
of hierarchical systems.

41
00:01:53,930 --> 00:01:56,450
So the task that we'll
be talking about today

42
00:01:56,450 --> 00:01:58,990
are long-horizon tasks.

43
00:01:58,990 --> 00:02:00,470
And by long-horizon
tasks, I mean

44
00:02:00,470 --> 00:02:03,070
tasks that take a
while to complete

45
00:02:03,070 --> 00:02:06,725
and might consist of a
number of different subtasks

46
00:02:06,725 --> 00:02:08,350
or a number of
different steps that you

47
00:02:08,350 --> 00:02:11,250
need to take in some
sort of sequence or order

48
00:02:11,250 --> 00:02:13,870
in order to complete
it successfully.

49
00:02:13,870 --> 00:02:17,030
So this could be something
like cooking a meal.

50
00:02:17,030 --> 00:02:22,290
It could be for a robot
system, for an LLM agent.

51
00:02:22,290 --> 00:02:26,510
It could be trying to fix a bug
that's causing a neural network

52
00:02:26,510 --> 00:02:28,350
training loss to explode.

53
00:02:28,350 --> 00:02:31,550
For an autonomous vehicle, it
could be driving to Yosemite.

54
00:02:31,550 --> 00:02:35,430
For a different LLM, it
could be giving feedback

55
00:02:35,430 --> 00:02:37,693
on a long report.

56
00:02:37,693 --> 00:02:39,110
And for these
tasks, you can think

57
00:02:39,110 --> 00:02:41,110
about how there are
oftentimes different steps

58
00:02:41,110 --> 00:02:42,390
that you need to complete.

59
00:02:42,390 --> 00:02:46,730
Some of those steps might be
interdependent on one another,

60
00:02:46,730 --> 00:02:49,150
where it makes sense to
do one before the other.

61
00:02:49,150 --> 00:02:51,270
And other tasks
might be something

62
00:02:51,270 --> 00:02:53,130
where they're less
interdependent,

63
00:02:53,130 --> 00:02:57,800
and it's actually OK to
do those in parallel.

64
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:02,760
These are really hard problems
for AI systems to solve,

65
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:03,330
in general.

66
00:03:03,330 --> 00:03:06,280

67
00:03:06,280 --> 00:03:07,860
There's a number of
reasons for that.

68
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:11,360
So the first is
that when you are

69
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:13,400
doing a task that
takes a long time,

70
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:17,760
you're visiting a large
number of states along the way

71
00:03:17,760 --> 00:03:19,040
to get there.

72
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:21,140
And every new state
that you visit,

73
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:24,160
you need to be able
to make decisions for.

74
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:27,320
So you basically just have
a much broader distribution

75
00:03:27,320 --> 00:03:31,280
that your policy needs
to be successful on.

76
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:34,720
There's also a lot of different
opportunities to make mistakes.

77
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:36,600
And if you make a
mistake, you need

78
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:38,520
to be able to recover
from that mistake,

79
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:40,742
if it's something that you
can actually recover from.

80
00:03:40,742 --> 00:03:42,200
And separate from
mistakes, there's

81
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:44,600
also just opportunities
to get stuck.

82
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:48,080
For example, if there's
one kind of mistake

83
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:49,800
that might, for
example, not lead

84
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:52,320
you to actually make
progress on the task.

85
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:55,220
And if you're not
making progress,

86
00:03:55,220 --> 00:03:58,580
then you'll actually just end
up in the same state again.

87
00:03:58,580 --> 00:04:01,580
And you'll keep on making that
same mistake over and over again

88
00:04:01,580 --> 00:04:04,860
and essentially just get
stuck at performing the task.

89
00:04:04,860 --> 00:04:07,140
And these kinds of
mistakes are particularly

90
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:11,160
gnarly because when you end
up in the same state again,

91
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:13,620
your policy will keep on
deterministically staying

92
00:04:13,620 --> 00:04:15,340
in that state and
never making forward

93
00:04:15,340 --> 00:04:18,140
progress towards the goal.

94
00:04:18,140 --> 00:04:21,240
And then what's the main
idea behind hierarchy?

95
00:04:21,240 --> 00:04:27,020
So we've talked about multi-task
policies, where you have some--

96
00:04:27,020 --> 00:04:30,100
actually, let me draw
this further to the right.

97
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:36,860
We have some policy that
takes as input some state.

98
00:04:36,860 --> 00:04:39,020
Maybe the state
at some timestep.

99
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:42,940
And it also takes as input
some task or some goal.

100
00:04:42,940 --> 00:04:44,600
I'm going to write
this as g for now

101
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:46,940
because this task
or goal could take

102
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:49,060
a number of different forms.

103
00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:52,380
And this is passed
into your policy

104
00:04:52,380 --> 00:04:56,470
to produce actions that
are trying to address

105
00:04:56,470 --> 00:04:57,770
that particular goal.

106
00:04:57,770 --> 00:05:00,350

107
00:05:00,350 --> 00:05:02,870
The idea for trying to
solve long-horizon tasks

108
00:05:02,870 --> 00:05:07,310
is if we have a policy that can
do a number of different things.

109
00:05:07,310 --> 00:05:10,790
Then we could try
to break up a bigger

110
00:05:10,790 --> 00:05:16,070
task into different
subtasks and then

111
00:05:16,070 --> 00:05:20,990
use one policy to actually tell
this policy what task it should

112
00:05:20,990 --> 00:05:25,550
be doing at any
given point in time.

113
00:05:25,550 --> 00:05:31,950
So for example, you could
have another policy here

114
00:05:31,950 --> 00:05:36,030
that's actually outputting
the goals to be doing.

115
00:05:36,030 --> 00:05:38,750
And this policy should
also have information

116
00:05:38,750 --> 00:05:42,150
about the state as input so
that it knows where it is

117
00:05:42,150 --> 00:05:48,190
and what goal makes sense
for the agent to do.

118
00:05:48,190 --> 00:05:52,990
And then optionally, this is
often called a low-level policy,

119
00:05:52,990 --> 00:05:55,990
or we'll use pi LL to refer.

120
00:05:55,990 --> 00:05:57,390
This is the low-level policy.

121
00:05:57,390 --> 00:05:59,070
This is doing shorter tasks.

122
00:05:59,070 --> 00:06:02,170
And we can refer to this as
a high-level policy that's

123
00:06:02,170 --> 00:06:06,450
outputting goals for the
low-level policy to accomplish.

124
00:06:06,450 --> 00:06:08,990
And this policy,
it can have a task.

125
00:06:08,990 --> 00:06:09,490
Sorry.

126
00:06:09,490 --> 00:06:10,590
I wrote this as low level.

127
00:06:10,590 --> 00:06:11,673
This should be high level.

128
00:06:11,673 --> 00:06:14,330

129
00:06:14,330 --> 00:06:16,490
If you want your
policy to be able to do

130
00:06:16,490 --> 00:06:19,890
multiple long-horizon tasks,
then this high-level policy

131
00:06:19,890 --> 00:06:23,490
could also have some sort of
task descriptor as input as

132
00:06:23,490 --> 00:06:25,490
well, or some sort
of goal as input.

133
00:06:25,490 --> 00:06:30,330
And so we, for example, could
have goal high level as input

134
00:06:30,330 --> 00:06:33,090
to this policy, if you
want to be completing

135
00:06:33,090 --> 00:06:35,990
not just one long-horizon task
but multiple long-horizon tasks.

136
00:06:35,990 --> 00:06:37,990
Like not just
driving to Yosemite

137
00:06:37,990 --> 00:06:40,730
but also being able
to drive to the Grand

138
00:06:40,730 --> 00:06:46,770
Canyon or LA or Seattle
or something like that.

139
00:06:46,770 --> 00:06:50,570
And the kind of crux
underlying this idea

140
00:06:50,570 --> 00:06:53,780
is that if we can break up a
really long task into smaller

141
00:06:53,780 --> 00:07:00,460
parts, then we might be able
to better address the problem.

142
00:07:00,460 --> 00:07:03,700
And say, for example,
your long-horizon task

143
00:07:03,700 --> 00:07:05,337
is to bake a cheesecake.

144
00:07:05,337 --> 00:07:06,920
This is a really,
really hard problem.

145
00:07:06,920 --> 00:07:10,620
And if you just tried to train
a policy like a robot policy

146
00:07:10,620 --> 00:07:13,460
to output motor commands
for doing this task,

147
00:07:13,460 --> 00:07:15,780
it's really hard to figure
out what you should be doing

148
00:07:15,780 --> 00:07:17,940
at any given point in time.

149
00:07:17,940 --> 00:07:20,565
But if you're just starting
out, you can think about, OK,

150
00:07:20,565 --> 00:07:22,440
what's the first step
for accomplishing this?

151
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:23,857
Maybe you need to
buy ingredients.

152
00:07:23,857 --> 00:07:25,898
Then what's the first step
of buying ingredients?

153
00:07:25,898 --> 00:07:27,565
Well, maybe you need
to go to the store.

154
00:07:27,565 --> 00:07:28,898
And how do you get to the store?

155
00:07:28,898 --> 00:07:30,660
Well, maybe you need
to walk to the door.

156
00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:33,362
To walk to the door,
you might take a step.

157
00:07:33,362 --> 00:07:34,320
How do you take a step?

158
00:07:34,320 --> 00:07:36,500
You might contract a muscle.

159
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:39,180
And so you can basically
break down a bigger task

160
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:40,620
into a much smaller task.

161
00:07:40,620 --> 00:07:45,140
And these smaller tasks are
a lot easier to train systems

162
00:07:45,140 --> 00:07:45,820
to do.

163
00:07:45,820 --> 00:07:48,200
And if you can train systems
to do the smaller tasks,

164
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:52,070
then you can try to compose
them with a higher-level policy

165
00:07:52,070 --> 00:07:55,100
to be able to accomplish
that more challenging task.

166
00:07:55,100 --> 00:07:57,870

167
00:07:57,870 --> 00:08:01,970
So what we can do is for the
"bake a cheesecake" task,

168
00:08:01,970 --> 00:08:05,190
we could pass this with a state
or observation into a high-level

169
00:08:05,190 --> 00:08:09,190
policy, which is going to
predict an intermediate step,

170
00:08:09,190 --> 00:08:13,230
like for example, go to the
store or go to the door.

171
00:08:13,230 --> 00:08:15,590
And then that intermediate
step, along with the state

172
00:08:15,590 --> 00:08:18,430
or observation, will be
passed to the low-level policy

173
00:08:18,430 --> 00:08:22,150
to predict an action.

174
00:08:22,150 --> 00:08:24,470
Now, a couple notes here.

175
00:08:24,470 --> 00:08:30,670
So one note is that
this low-level policy--

176
00:08:30,670 --> 00:08:35,650
this needs to be acting
at the original frequency.

177
00:08:35,650 --> 00:08:39,590
So if you're
controlling a robot,

178
00:08:39,590 --> 00:08:41,325
maybe this is
operating at 20 hertz.

179
00:08:41,325 --> 00:08:42,950
If you're controlling
a language model,

180
00:08:42,950 --> 00:08:45,670
then this is going to be
outputting word by word.

181
00:08:45,670 --> 00:08:47,270
Whereas the
high-level policy, it

182
00:08:47,270 --> 00:08:50,810
could actually predict goals
for the low-level policy

183
00:08:50,810 --> 00:08:53,010
at a coarser timescale.

184
00:08:53,010 --> 00:08:55,690
And so, for example, it
could be outputting goals

185
00:08:55,690 --> 00:08:58,610
after you've completed a
paragraph for a language model

186
00:08:58,610 --> 00:09:01,530
or after you've completed that--

187
00:09:01,530 --> 00:09:03,950
like going to the
store, for example.

188
00:09:03,950 --> 00:09:06,810
It could then give a new
goal for the low-level policy

189
00:09:06,810 --> 00:09:08,385
to perform.

190
00:09:08,385 --> 00:09:10,010
And so we have a
high-level policy that

191
00:09:10,010 --> 00:09:11,352
predicts intermediate goals.

192
00:09:11,352 --> 00:09:12,810
And then the low
level policy tries

193
00:09:12,810 --> 00:09:17,290
to accomplish those intermediate
goals by predicting actions.

194
00:09:17,290 --> 00:09:18,930
Typically, the
low-level policy is

195
00:09:18,930 --> 00:09:23,490
operating at a higher frequency
than the high-level policy.

196
00:09:23,490 --> 00:09:26,502
And I should note that--

197
00:09:26,502 --> 00:09:30,010
I'll refer to these as
kind of goals or subtasks.

198
00:09:30,010 --> 00:09:32,290
There's a number of
other names for these

199
00:09:32,290 --> 00:09:34,690
as well, like skills or options.

200
00:09:34,690 --> 00:09:37,270
You could also think about
these as high-level actions.

201
00:09:37,270 --> 00:09:39,850
So these are actions but
for a different policy

202
00:09:39,850 --> 00:09:42,450
that's operating at a
higher level of abstraction

203
00:09:42,450 --> 00:09:44,820
than the original policy.

204
00:09:44,820 --> 00:09:47,330

205
00:09:47,330 --> 00:09:49,460
And then the other
thing here is that--

206
00:09:49,460 --> 00:09:51,820
this is like a
two-level hierarchy,

207
00:09:51,820 --> 00:09:55,260
but you could also imagine
having more than two levels

208
00:09:55,260 --> 00:09:56,800
of hierarchy as well.

209
00:09:56,800 --> 00:09:59,500
And for example, the baking a
cheesecake, you could have--

210
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:02,380
maybe one level
of hierarchy could

211
00:10:02,380 --> 00:10:08,923
be things like go to the store,
buy the things, bring them back.

212
00:10:08,923 --> 00:10:10,340
And a different
level of hierarchy

213
00:10:10,340 --> 00:10:13,420
might actually be controlling,
like the motors on a robot,

214
00:10:13,420 --> 00:10:17,700
in order to accomplish
those tasks.

215
00:10:17,700 --> 00:10:20,520
And then once we have
this hierarchical policy,

216
00:10:20,520 --> 00:10:23,800
how do we actually roll it
out if we can do these things?

217
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:29,340
So specifically, if we're at,
say, we'll start at t equals 1,

218
00:10:29,340 --> 00:10:35,220
then the first
step is to predict.

219
00:10:35,220 --> 00:10:39,440
Or another way to think about
predicting a goal is planning.

220
00:10:39,440 --> 00:10:41,153
We're trying to think forward.

221
00:10:41,153 --> 00:10:42,820
What is the next step
that we should do?

222
00:10:42,820 --> 00:10:45,140
So we could try to plan a goal.

223
00:10:45,140 --> 00:10:50,280
And this will be by sampling
from our high-level policy based

224
00:10:50,280 --> 00:10:59,000
off of the current state and
optionally some high-level goal.

225
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:02,160
And then we'll be
repeatedly sampling

226
00:11:02,160 --> 00:11:04,480
from our low-level policy.

227
00:11:04,480 --> 00:11:08,960
And so we could repeat.

228
00:11:08,960 --> 00:11:16,240
First sample, an action from the
low-level policy using this goal

229
00:11:16,240 --> 00:11:17,360
as input.

230
00:11:17,360 --> 00:11:27,400
So sample a from high low level,
with the state and this goal--

231
00:11:27,400 --> 00:11:29,640
the goal from the
high level as input.

232
00:11:29,640 --> 00:11:33,280
And then we'll actually
execute that action

233
00:11:33,280 --> 00:11:35,050
and get the next state.

234
00:11:35,050 --> 00:11:39,920

235
00:11:39,920 --> 00:11:42,772
So basically at that
point, we'll then--

236
00:11:42,772 --> 00:11:46,210
t will now become t plus 1.

237
00:11:46,210 --> 00:11:51,250
And we'll observe St. Then again
with the same goal as before.

238
00:11:51,250 --> 00:11:54,250
But the new state will then
be sampling a new action,

239
00:11:54,250 --> 00:11:56,650
repeating this process.

240
00:11:56,650 --> 00:11:59,250
And then at some
point after we have--

241
00:11:59,250 --> 00:12:01,010
and we'll talk a
little bit about how we

242
00:12:01,010 --> 00:12:02,420
decide when to get a new goal.

243
00:12:02,420 --> 00:12:04,170
But when we have decided
that we're either

244
00:12:04,170 --> 00:12:06,530
done with the goal or
enough time has passed,

245
00:12:06,530 --> 00:12:11,610
then we'll move back to step one
to re-plan a new goal based off

246
00:12:11,610 --> 00:12:14,010
of the latest state.

247
00:12:14,010 --> 00:12:16,307
Yeah.

248
00:12:16,307 --> 00:12:18,890
So let's walk through this, and
then I'll get to your question

249
00:12:18,890 --> 00:12:19,470
in a minute.

250
00:12:19,470 --> 00:12:23,410
So is there some initial state
or observation, plan a new goal?

251
00:12:23,410 --> 00:12:24,890
Until a new goal
is selected, we're

252
00:12:24,890 --> 00:12:27,410
going to execute a
predicted action according

253
00:12:27,410 --> 00:12:30,650
to our low-level policy based
off of that predicted goal.

254
00:12:30,650 --> 00:12:34,050
And then we'll observe
a new observation.

255
00:12:34,050 --> 00:12:36,630
Now, why might hierarchy help?

256
00:12:36,630 --> 00:12:39,050
Which I think is getting
at your question.

257
00:12:39,050 --> 00:12:40,990
There's a couple
reasons for this.

258
00:12:40,990 --> 00:12:45,390
So the first is
that typically we're

259
00:12:45,390 --> 00:12:47,790
going to be training this
low-level policy to reach

260
00:12:47,790 --> 00:12:51,430
goals and this high-level policy
to maximize rewards or reach

261
00:12:51,430 --> 00:12:52,950
some high-level goals.

262
00:12:52,950 --> 00:12:57,110
And this means that the
objective for this policy

263
00:12:57,110 --> 00:13:00,470
is different than the overall
objective of completing

264
00:13:00,470 --> 00:13:02,030
the long-horizon task.

265
00:13:02,030 --> 00:13:04,290
And so this is actually
getting different supervision.

266
00:13:04,290 --> 00:13:05,957
And this intermediate
supervision signal

267
00:13:05,957 --> 00:13:08,790
for how to complete
intermediate goals or tasks

268
00:13:08,790 --> 00:13:11,910
can be useful for guiding
the policy towards completing

269
00:13:11,910 --> 00:13:13,350
the long-horizon task.

270
00:13:13,350 --> 00:13:14,945
So you can think
about it potentially.

271
00:13:14,945 --> 00:13:17,070
It depends on the setup,
but you can think about it

272
00:13:17,070 --> 00:13:20,107
as a way to leverage
intermediate supervision.

273
00:13:20,107 --> 00:13:22,190
Now you could always have
intermediate supervision

274
00:13:22,190 --> 00:13:24,110
when training a big, flat
policy as well, where

275
00:13:24,110 --> 00:13:25,902
you give it shaped
rewards, and you give it

276
00:13:25,902 --> 00:13:28,880
rewards for completing
individual subtasks.

277
00:13:28,880 --> 00:13:30,630
But compared to that,
the other difference

278
00:13:30,630 --> 00:13:34,350
here is that this
low-level multi-task policy

279
00:13:34,350 --> 00:13:38,990
is going to be able to more
explicitly share knowledge

280
00:13:38,990 --> 00:13:41,350
across the different subtasks.

281
00:13:41,350 --> 00:13:47,440
So because, for example,
it has this goal as input,

282
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:51,800
it's targeting a
particular subtask.

283
00:13:51,800 --> 00:13:55,880
And if two subtasks are
quite similar to one another,

284
00:13:55,880 --> 00:14:01,840
then this policy will be able
to share data across those tasks

285
00:14:01,840 --> 00:14:03,420
in order to more
efficiently learn,

286
00:14:03,420 --> 00:14:04,878
compared to if you
were just to try

287
00:14:04,878 --> 00:14:08,640
to train a single policy
across the long-horizon task.

288
00:14:08,640 --> 00:14:10,680
In reinforcement learning,
specifically, you

289
00:14:10,680 --> 00:14:14,680
can also think about benefits
in terms of exploration.

290
00:14:14,680 --> 00:14:18,920
And in particular, when
this high-level policy

291
00:14:18,920 --> 00:14:21,320
is proposing goals
to reach, that's

292
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:23,000
structuring the
exploration process

293
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:26,040
to encourage it to explore not
just in the original action

294
00:14:26,040 --> 00:14:30,920
space but also in this
higher-level goal space.

295
00:14:30,920 --> 00:14:35,960
And lastly, there can also be
sometimes practical benefits

296
00:14:35,960 --> 00:14:36,462
as well.

297
00:14:36,462 --> 00:14:37,920
So, for example,
if you're thinking

298
00:14:37,920 --> 00:14:40,647
about this task of making
a cheesecake, for example,

299
00:14:40,647 --> 00:14:42,980
you might need to be reasoning
through the steps for how

300
00:14:42,980 --> 00:14:43,960
to do that.

301
00:14:43,960 --> 00:14:46,580
And if you have a
single policy, then you

302
00:14:46,580 --> 00:14:48,300
need to reason
through those steps

303
00:14:48,300 --> 00:14:52,580
every single timestep
with that flat policy.

304
00:14:52,580 --> 00:14:54,600
So if your robot is
operating at 20 hertz,

305
00:14:54,600 --> 00:14:57,100
for example, you're going to
be reasoning through like, what

306
00:14:57,100 --> 00:14:58,560
is the next thing
I should be doing

307
00:14:58,560 --> 00:15:02,140
and the way to get there with my
action at every single timestep?

308
00:15:02,140 --> 00:15:05,260
Where here, we can run
this reasoning computation

309
00:15:05,260 --> 00:15:09,340
at a lower frequency than
the low-level policy.

310
00:15:09,340 --> 00:15:11,340
So that can be kind
of a practical benefit

311
00:15:11,340 --> 00:15:13,640
in terms of compute costs.

312
00:15:13,640 --> 00:15:14,140
Yeah.

313
00:15:14,140 --> 00:15:17,380
So the question is, can we
elaborate on the first point,

314
00:15:17,380 --> 00:15:19,580
and how the supervision
for the low-level policy

315
00:15:19,580 --> 00:15:21,420
is different for the
high-level policy?

316
00:15:21,420 --> 00:15:23,940
So the high level policy--

317
00:15:23,940 --> 00:15:26,860
it's goal is to complete
the long-horizon task.

318
00:15:26,860 --> 00:15:29,940
So it's goal is to
bake the cheesecake,

319
00:15:29,940 --> 00:15:32,380
and it gets rewards when
the cheesecake is baked.

320
00:15:32,380 --> 00:15:35,980
The low-level policy--
it gets rewards

321
00:15:35,980 --> 00:15:38,460
for completing
intermediate subtasks.

322
00:15:38,460 --> 00:15:42,853
And so when it, for example,
finds the ingredients, preheat

323
00:15:42,853 --> 00:15:44,270
the oven, that
sort of thing, it's

324
00:15:44,270 --> 00:15:46,990
getting rewards for
completing those tasks.

325
00:15:46,990 --> 00:15:48,990
And that intermediate
supervision

326
00:15:48,990 --> 00:15:51,970
guides it towards completing
the long-horizon task.

327
00:15:51,970 --> 00:15:52,470
Yeah.

328
00:15:52,470 --> 00:15:54,220
So the question is,
what if you need to be

329
00:15:54,220 --> 00:15:56,310
doing multiple tasks at once?

330
00:15:56,310 --> 00:16:02,617
So in that case, unless your
low-level agent can has--

331
00:16:02,617 --> 00:16:04,950
I don't know-- 10 arms or
something or can output things

332
00:16:04,950 --> 00:16:08,830
in parallel, you do need to
pick some order for that.

333
00:16:08,830 --> 00:16:11,870
Now, the goal of the
high-level policy

334
00:16:11,870 --> 00:16:14,450
is to figure out whether
that order matters or not.

335
00:16:14,450 --> 00:16:18,170
And so if it figures out that
the order is really important,

336
00:16:18,170 --> 00:16:20,345
then it will get rewards.

337
00:16:20,345 --> 00:16:22,470
It will be better able to
predict those goals if it

338
00:16:22,470 --> 00:16:23,990
predicts the correct order.

339
00:16:23,990 --> 00:16:26,330
In other circumstances, if
the order doesn't matter,

340
00:16:26,330 --> 00:16:31,430
then it should be able to
learn that and figure out

341
00:16:31,430 --> 00:16:33,750
that it doesn't really
matter, and it'll just

342
00:16:33,750 --> 00:16:36,910
pick one arbitrarily.

343
00:16:36,910 --> 00:16:40,960
Now, there's also a
question of whether or not

344
00:16:40,960 --> 00:16:43,040
we actually need multiple
policies in order

345
00:16:43,040 --> 00:16:45,120
to get some of these benefits.

346
00:16:45,120 --> 00:16:48,840
And on the last slide, we
talked about the benefits

347
00:16:48,840 --> 00:16:51,320
of what you might get with
a hierarchical approach

348
00:16:51,320 --> 00:16:54,760
versus just what's called a flat
policy with no hierarchy, which

349
00:16:54,760 --> 00:16:59,440
is the typical path in the
long-horizon task and the state

350
00:16:59,440 --> 00:17:01,680
and predict the action.

351
00:17:01,680 --> 00:17:03,680
There's actually also
an intermediate approach

352
00:17:03,680 --> 00:17:06,440
that you can take as
well, that actually

353
00:17:06,440 --> 00:17:08,680
retains a lot of the
benefits of hierarchy

354
00:17:08,680 --> 00:17:11,079
and actually, in some ways,
has a lot of the same design

355
00:17:11,079 --> 00:17:14,520
choices, which is something
that's a little bit like chain

356
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:18,280
of thought in language models,
if you're familiar with that--

357
00:17:18,280 --> 00:17:21,520
which is that if you have
some long-horizon task,

358
00:17:21,520 --> 00:17:26,319
you could have the policy
first reason through what goal

359
00:17:26,319 --> 00:17:29,800
or what subtask it should be
completing and then predict

360
00:17:29,800 --> 00:17:31,400
the low-level action.

361
00:17:31,400 --> 00:17:36,960
So this is kind of combining
these two policies into one.

362
00:17:36,960 --> 00:17:39,080
But now you're just
predicting them in sequence.

363
00:17:39,080 --> 00:17:42,140
You're first predicting the
goal and then the action.

364
00:17:42,140 --> 00:17:43,580
This sort of
approach can actually

365
00:17:43,580 --> 00:17:45,520
retain a fair number
of the benefits,

366
00:17:45,520 --> 00:17:47,020
so it can benefit
from the same sort

367
00:17:47,020 --> 00:17:49,460
of intermediate supervision.

368
00:17:49,460 --> 00:17:51,560
It also might have some
practical downsides.

369
00:17:51,560 --> 00:17:53,740
So this doesn't
have the benefits

370
00:17:53,740 --> 00:17:56,577
of the kind of practical
computational benefits

371
00:17:56,577 --> 00:17:58,660
of separating them into
two because then you still

372
00:17:58,660 --> 00:18:02,020
have to run this at
the full frequency.

373
00:18:02,020 --> 00:18:04,540
There isn't entirely
a conclusion

374
00:18:04,540 --> 00:18:07,940
yet empirically on
whether this is just

375
00:18:07,940 --> 00:18:09,557
as good as a
hierarchical approach.

376
00:18:09,557 --> 00:18:11,140
And you could imagine
that there might

377
00:18:11,140 --> 00:18:13,660
be benefits in terms of
sharing some knowledge

378
00:18:13,660 --> 00:18:16,580
with low-level policies versus
with a combined approach.

379
00:18:16,580 --> 00:18:18,300
So this is an open
area of research

380
00:18:18,300 --> 00:18:20,700
on whether or not you
could just do this.

381
00:18:20,700 --> 00:18:22,740
And the only really
conclusive downside

382
00:18:22,740 --> 00:18:28,540
would be the computational
upside of having reasoning

383
00:18:28,540 --> 00:18:32,603
at a lower frequency than taking
actions for the first one.

384
00:18:32,603 --> 00:18:35,020
So the question is, why is
having intermediate supervision

385
00:18:35,020 --> 00:18:36,950
helpful?

386
00:18:36,950 --> 00:18:38,530
There's a couple reasons.

387
00:18:38,530 --> 00:18:40,590
So if you're in a
reinforcement learning setting

388
00:18:40,590 --> 00:18:42,470
and your goal is to
make a cheesecake

389
00:18:42,470 --> 00:18:46,070
and you only get rewards when
the cheesecake is baked versus

390
00:18:46,070 --> 00:18:49,190
not, then those rewards are
going to be really sparse

391
00:18:49,190 --> 00:18:52,290
and not actually
allow you to progress.

392
00:18:52,290 --> 00:18:54,790
The second is that even if you--
say, you're in an imitation

393
00:18:54,790 --> 00:19:02,110
learning setting, like you're
just predicting actions,

394
00:19:02,110 --> 00:19:04,310
imitating actions for
making a cheesecake,

395
00:19:04,310 --> 00:19:08,737
then sometimes if the
policy isn't explicitly

396
00:19:08,737 --> 00:19:11,070
thinking about what part of
that task it might be doing,

397
00:19:11,070 --> 00:19:14,370
it might oscillate between
different subtasks,

398
00:19:14,370 --> 00:19:18,950
especially if it doesn't have
a history of past observations.

399
00:19:18,950 --> 00:19:23,070
So there's also even benefits
in imitation learning settings

400
00:19:23,070 --> 00:19:24,750
as well.

401
00:19:24,750 --> 00:19:27,430
So now let's dive into
some of the design choices.

402
00:19:27,430 --> 00:19:30,190
This will also start to answer
some of the questions that

403
00:19:30,190 --> 00:19:31,870
have come up as well.

404
00:19:31,870 --> 00:19:35,470
So the first is that
we've abstractly

405
00:19:35,470 --> 00:19:38,450
been talking about
these different goals,

406
00:19:38,450 --> 00:19:42,050
and we need to think about
how to actually represent

407
00:19:42,050 --> 00:19:44,450
those different goals.

408
00:19:44,450 --> 00:19:49,490
And I'm actually want to start
with this as an exercise for you

409
00:19:49,490 --> 00:19:53,770
all to think about what
different representations might

410
00:19:53,770 --> 00:19:55,740
make sense for
different problems.

411
00:19:55,740 --> 00:19:57,990
And so I'll give you a few
different example problems.

412
00:19:57,990 --> 00:20:00,290
And then the goal for
you is to think through

413
00:20:00,290 --> 00:20:02,090
for that particular problem.

414
00:20:02,090 --> 00:20:05,130
What would be kind of
sensible intermediate steps

415
00:20:05,130 --> 00:20:06,790
for the agent?

416
00:20:06,790 --> 00:20:08,290
And how would you
actually represent

417
00:20:08,290 --> 00:20:10,290
those different
intermediate steps?

418
00:20:10,290 --> 00:20:13,050
So the first task
is a robot that

419
00:20:13,050 --> 00:20:17,170
wants to learn how to cook
Italian dishes like focaccia.

420
00:20:17,170 --> 00:20:20,450
What would the intermediate
steps for those cooking tasks

421
00:20:20,450 --> 00:20:23,410
look like if it needs to
control its motors to complete

422
00:20:23,410 --> 00:20:26,450
that task and the
representation for that?

423
00:20:26,450 --> 00:20:30,490
The second one is maybe you're
developing an autonomous driving

424
00:20:30,490 --> 00:20:35,740
bike, and you want it to break
down navigation and biking

425
00:20:35,740 --> 00:20:38,460
to various locations on campus.

426
00:20:38,460 --> 00:20:40,540
The third is a
language model that

427
00:20:40,540 --> 00:20:44,300
is drafting legal briefs,
which is also a rather

428
00:20:44,300 --> 00:20:46,160
complex and involved process.

429
00:20:46,160 --> 00:20:48,340
And then the last
is maybe a web agent

430
00:20:48,340 --> 00:20:54,140
that needs to plan and book
one week vacations for users.

431
00:20:54,140 --> 00:20:57,080
So I'll give you a couple
minutes to pick one of these.

432
00:20:57,080 --> 00:20:59,660
Discuss with your neighbor what
intermediate goals would look

433
00:20:59,660 --> 00:21:00,340
like.

434
00:21:00,340 --> 00:21:01,950
And then we'll come
back and discuss.

435
00:21:01,950 --> 00:21:05,500

436
00:21:05,500 --> 00:21:08,120
Does anyone want to share
what they discussed?

437
00:21:08,120 --> 00:21:08,620
Cool.

438
00:21:08,620 --> 00:21:09,120
Yeah.

439
00:21:09,120 --> 00:21:10,900
So for number two,
one way that you

440
00:21:10,900 --> 00:21:13,067
could address it is the
different intermediate goals

441
00:21:13,067 --> 00:21:18,100
could be like waypoints towards
that particular goal location

442
00:21:18,100 --> 00:21:19,160
that you want to reach.

443
00:21:19,160 --> 00:21:21,760
And then the representation for
that could be GPS coordinates.

444
00:21:21,760 --> 00:21:23,385
And the reward for
the low-level policy

445
00:21:23,385 --> 00:21:26,700
would be like, how close are
you to those GPS coordinates?

446
00:21:26,700 --> 00:21:28,980
So yeah, something
like that makes sense?

447
00:21:28,980 --> 00:21:32,980
Anyone else want to share
either for that problem

448
00:21:32,980 --> 00:21:34,880
or a different problem?

449
00:21:34,880 --> 00:21:35,580
Yeah, cool.

450
00:21:35,580 --> 00:21:37,680
So for the fourth one,
you could-- there's

451
00:21:37,680 --> 00:21:39,020
a few different ways you
could think about it.

452
00:21:39,020 --> 00:21:41,395
You could think about it as
intermediate goals or booking

453
00:21:41,395 --> 00:21:43,440
day one, booking day
two, booking day three.

454
00:21:43,440 --> 00:21:46,023
Or you could have a more coarse,
defined approach where you're

455
00:21:46,023 --> 00:21:49,400
booking travel, booking--

456
00:21:49,400 --> 00:21:51,440
I don't know-- hotels
or whatever, and then

457
00:21:51,440 --> 00:21:52,922
specific activities.

458
00:21:52,922 --> 00:21:54,880
One other part of this
that I would imagine you

459
00:21:54,880 --> 00:21:57,040
would need to do is just
generally figuring out

460
00:21:57,040 --> 00:21:59,140
what does the user want
from their vacation.

461
00:21:59,140 --> 00:22:02,200
And so doing high-level
research first.

462
00:22:02,200 --> 00:22:05,200
And that could be also
an intermediate goal

463
00:22:05,200 --> 00:22:08,080
as part of completing the task.

464
00:22:08,080 --> 00:22:10,597
Did anyone pick one or three?

465
00:22:10,597 --> 00:22:13,180
So the intermediate goals are
things like opening the cabinet,

466
00:22:13,180 --> 00:22:15,763
beating the eggs, and then the
representation for those goals.

467
00:22:15,763 --> 00:22:21,786

468
00:22:21,786 --> 00:22:22,540
All right.

469
00:22:22,540 --> 00:22:24,065
Didn't quite get to that.

470
00:22:24,065 --> 00:22:25,440
So a few things
that you could do

471
00:22:25,440 --> 00:22:26,920
for the representation
for that is it

472
00:22:26,920 --> 00:22:28,295
could just be a
natural language.

473
00:22:28,295 --> 00:22:31,040
So it could be literally
the string beat the eggs

474
00:22:31,040 --> 00:22:32,970
or open the cabinet.

475
00:22:32,970 --> 00:22:35,038
If, for example, if
you're cooking something

476
00:22:35,038 --> 00:22:36,830
where you want to look
in a particular way,

477
00:22:36,830 --> 00:22:40,210
you can even have an image as an
intermediate goal representation

478
00:22:40,210 --> 00:22:43,730
as well, saying like, I want
the bread to look like this,

479
00:22:43,730 --> 00:22:45,470
or yeah, that sort of thing.

480
00:22:45,470 --> 00:22:50,930
So can imagine multimodal
goal representations as well.

481
00:22:50,930 --> 00:22:53,930
So one of the
things that I wanted

482
00:22:53,930 --> 00:22:57,410
to convey with this exercise
is that oftentimes the goal

483
00:22:57,410 --> 00:23:01,490
representation that
you might pick likely

484
00:23:01,490 --> 00:23:04,462
depends on the particular domain
or the particular problem.

485
00:23:04,462 --> 00:23:06,170
For example, like for
biking, it might be

486
00:23:06,170 --> 00:23:07,850
something like GPS coordinates.

487
00:23:07,850 --> 00:23:10,210
Whereas for cooking,
it might be language.

488
00:23:10,210 --> 00:23:13,690
For booking a vacation, it
might also be in language.

489
00:23:13,690 --> 00:23:16,890
There might also be images
involved and so forth.

490
00:23:16,890 --> 00:23:20,890
And in terms of what these
representations might look like,

491
00:23:20,890 --> 00:23:24,250
oftentimes you'll
want it to be--

492
00:23:24,250 --> 00:23:27,290
if you're developing something
for many different subtasks,

493
00:23:27,290 --> 00:23:28,790
you want it to be
fairly expressive.

494
00:23:28,790 --> 00:23:32,203
If you're trying to cook
lots of different meals--

495
00:23:32,203 --> 00:23:34,620
natural language is something
that's like quite expressive

496
00:23:34,620 --> 00:23:39,420
and can describe a number of
different low-level behaviors.

497
00:23:39,420 --> 00:23:42,100
Ideally, it also
has some structure.

498
00:23:42,100 --> 00:23:44,820
And so for example, if your
different goals are just

499
00:23:44,820 --> 00:23:47,540
do task subtask
one, do subtask two,

500
00:23:47,540 --> 00:23:49,580
it's going to be hard
for your low-level policy

501
00:23:49,580 --> 00:23:52,020
to figure out how those
relate to one another.

502
00:23:52,020 --> 00:23:54,380
Whereas if you have something
like GPS coordinates,

503
00:23:54,380 --> 00:23:59,020
then two GPS coordinates that
are very close to each other

504
00:23:59,020 --> 00:24:00,520
are going to have
similar actions.

505
00:24:00,520 --> 00:24:02,820
And that sort of
structure will make

506
00:24:02,820 --> 00:24:08,100
the learning problem a lot
easier for the low-level policy.

507
00:24:08,100 --> 00:24:10,820
And then the third is also
picking an appropriate level

508
00:24:10,820 --> 00:24:12,020
of abstraction.

509
00:24:12,020 --> 00:24:17,300
So for the biking
task, for example,

510
00:24:17,300 --> 00:24:19,380
you can have a level
of abstraction that's

511
00:24:19,380 --> 00:24:22,160
like potentially two low level.

512
00:24:22,160 --> 00:24:26,860
So you could have something
that's kind of pedal--

513
00:24:26,860 --> 00:24:30,200
I don't know-- current,
like exact velocity

514
00:24:30,200 --> 00:24:31,400
for the next second.

515
00:24:31,400 --> 00:24:33,275
You could also have
something too high level,

516
00:24:33,275 --> 00:24:37,120
which is like go to the quad
or something, which might not

517
00:24:37,120 --> 00:24:39,720
be particularly helpful in terms
of breaking the problem down

518
00:24:39,720 --> 00:24:41,280
into smaller steps.

519
00:24:41,280 --> 00:24:43,960
And choosing the right
level of abstraction

520
00:24:43,960 --> 00:24:50,220
is often somewhat tricky and
can also be domain dependent,

521
00:24:50,220 --> 00:24:53,120
depending on the
particular kind of details

522
00:24:53,120 --> 00:24:57,128
of the problem and
the application area.

523
00:24:57,128 --> 00:24:58,920
So the question is,
should we also abstract

524
00:24:58,920 --> 00:25:02,440
the state space for each layer?

525
00:25:02,440 --> 00:25:07,480
This depends on whether or
not you have access to that--

526
00:25:07,480 --> 00:25:10,160
that sort of abstraction layer.

527
00:25:10,160 --> 00:25:14,160

528
00:25:14,160 --> 00:25:14,860
Let's see.

529
00:25:14,860 --> 00:25:16,997
Oftentimes, even for
the high-level policy,

530
00:25:16,997 --> 00:25:19,080
it's helpful for it to
have all of the information

531
00:25:19,080 --> 00:25:23,920
about the current state in order
to make even high-level goal

532
00:25:23,920 --> 00:25:26,000
predictions.

533
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:30,830
But yeah, there are some
things that you can do.

534
00:25:30,830 --> 00:25:32,850
You could, for example,
ask a language model

535
00:25:32,850 --> 00:25:35,850
to summarize the high-level
information about a state

536
00:25:35,850 --> 00:25:39,490
and have a high-level
policy use that information.

537
00:25:39,490 --> 00:25:43,610
But generally, in
practice, you'll

538
00:25:43,610 --> 00:25:47,510
often use the same low level,
like the full-state information.

539
00:25:47,510 --> 00:25:50,488
You don't lose too much
from doing that as well.

540
00:25:50,488 --> 00:25:52,530
Now let's talk a little
bit about the supervision

541
00:25:52,530 --> 00:25:53,430
for each level.

542
00:25:53,430 --> 00:25:59,410
And for this, I think it's
helpful to consider an example.

543
00:25:59,410 --> 00:26:08,050
So let's consider an example
where you're making pasta.

544
00:26:08,050 --> 00:26:12,440
And I chose this to be slightly
simpler long-horizon task.

545
00:26:12,440 --> 00:26:14,690
And you could imagine there
being a few steps involved

546
00:26:14,690 --> 00:26:16,090
for doing this.

547
00:26:16,090 --> 00:26:19,090
And we can abstractly
think about the state

548
00:26:19,090 --> 00:26:22,220
space for this task and reaching
different intermediate steps

549
00:26:22,220 --> 00:26:22,720
for this.

550
00:26:22,720 --> 00:26:26,870

551
00:26:26,870 --> 00:26:27,370
Let's see.

552
00:26:27,370 --> 00:26:32,430
Let's say that this is your
initial state distribution.

553
00:26:32,430 --> 00:26:35,030
And the steps for
making pasta is

554
00:26:35,030 --> 00:26:39,750
that you'll need to get
out a pot and pasta, so

555
00:26:39,750 --> 00:26:40,710
some ingredients.

556
00:26:40,710 --> 00:26:42,570
You'll need to boil the water.

557
00:26:42,570 --> 00:26:49,830
And you also need to pour the
pasta into the boiling water.

558
00:26:49,830 --> 00:26:51,330
Maybe there's also
a straining step,

559
00:26:51,330 --> 00:26:53,470
but maybe we can
skip that for now.

560
00:26:53,470 --> 00:26:56,500
And we can think about the--

561
00:26:56,500 --> 00:26:59,390

562
00:26:59,390 --> 00:27:02,690
imagine the set of
states where you have--

563
00:27:02,690 --> 00:27:06,930
for example, you
have taken out a pot.

564
00:27:06,930 --> 00:27:13,890
So find a pot before actually
like putting any water into it.

565
00:27:13,890 --> 00:27:16,310
You could have
some states, where

566
00:27:16,310 --> 00:27:23,070
you've now have boiled the
water, another set of states

567
00:27:23,070 --> 00:27:32,400
where you have found the pasta,
and then some other states where

568
00:27:32,400 --> 00:27:35,210
the pasta is cooked.

569
00:27:35,210 --> 00:27:42,040

570
00:27:42,040 --> 00:27:43,920
If we have our
low-level policy, it's

571
00:27:43,920 --> 00:27:46,320
going to be trying
to figure out like,

572
00:27:46,320 --> 00:27:49,705
how do I take actions to
reach these kinds of states?

573
00:27:49,705 --> 00:27:51,080
And then the
high-level policy is

574
00:27:51,080 --> 00:27:54,560
trying to predict, if
I'm in a given state,

575
00:27:54,560 --> 00:27:59,040
what command should I use to
tell the low-level policy what

576
00:27:59,040 --> 00:28:00,400
to do?

577
00:28:00,400 --> 00:28:02,520
And generally, as we've
talked about a bit

578
00:28:02,520 --> 00:28:06,080
before, for the
low-level policy,

579
00:28:06,080 --> 00:28:08,320
it's going to be getting
rewards that are based off

580
00:28:08,320 --> 00:28:11,560
of the low-level tasks.

581
00:28:11,560 --> 00:28:14,440
And the goal of
something like this

582
00:28:14,440 --> 00:28:18,220
would be something where you're
moving towards finding a pot.

583
00:28:18,220 --> 00:28:22,780
Maybe then you try
to boil the water.

584
00:28:22,780 --> 00:28:24,380
Then you try to find the pasta.

585
00:28:24,380 --> 00:28:27,700
And then you try
to cook the pasta.

586
00:28:27,700 --> 00:28:30,717
Now, one thing that we talked
about a little bit before is--

587
00:28:30,717 --> 00:28:33,300
so this is actually deviating
from this example a little bit--

588
00:28:33,300 --> 00:28:34,900
we'll come back to it--

589
00:28:34,900 --> 00:28:38,100
is we could also just
train the high-level policy

590
00:28:38,100 --> 00:28:40,420
and the low-level
policy end to end

591
00:28:40,420 --> 00:28:43,798
without any intermediate
goal representation.

592
00:28:43,798 --> 00:28:45,340
Just for example,
we'd have some sort

593
00:28:45,340 --> 00:28:46,920
of latent goal representation.

594
00:28:46,920 --> 00:28:49,340
That's just the activations
of the neural network.

595
00:28:49,340 --> 00:28:52,300
And we could train
all of these just

596
00:28:52,300 --> 00:28:57,260
for the high-level task and the
rewards of completing the task.

597
00:28:57,260 --> 00:28:59,580
Now, one important thing
that is good to know about

598
00:28:59,580 --> 00:29:03,140
of the supervision is that if
you do that, that's basically

599
00:29:03,140 --> 00:29:07,820
just a flat policy with a
particular architecture that

600
00:29:07,820 --> 00:29:12,020
has some sort of activations
when you feed in the observation

601
00:29:12,020 --> 00:29:12,520
twice.

602
00:29:12,520 --> 00:29:14,380
You just think about
that as basically

603
00:29:14,380 --> 00:29:16,900
a single neural
network architecture.

604
00:29:16,900 --> 00:29:21,500
That's basically the
same as a flat policy.

605
00:29:21,500 --> 00:29:24,390
And the reason why it's
important to recognize this is,

606
00:29:24,390 --> 00:29:27,252
is that when designing
these kinds of systems,

607
00:29:27,252 --> 00:29:29,710
it's important to think about
where the benefits are coming

608
00:29:29,710 --> 00:29:32,010
from, whether it's coming
from additional supervision,

609
00:29:32,010 --> 00:29:34,910
whether it's coming from
structured exploration.

610
00:29:34,910 --> 00:29:37,010
And if you don't think
carefully about this,

611
00:29:37,010 --> 00:29:39,036
you could end up in
a situation where

612
00:29:39,036 --> 00:29:41,538
intuitively if you haven't
thought about it, it's like, oh.

613
00:29:41,538 --> 00:29:43,330
Ideally, my high-level
policy will do this.

614
00:29:43,330 --> 00:29:46,390
My low-level policy will do this
without actually seeing benefits

615
00:29:46,390 --> 00:29:48,710
compared to just
naively training

616
00:29:48,710 --> 00:29:50,860
things with a single policy.

617
00:29:50,860 --> 00:29:53,262

618
00:29:53,262 --> 00:29:54,970
For those of you that
are doing research,

619
00:29:54,970 --> 00:29:57,470
it's also just generally
something that's good to do

620
00:29:57,470 --> 00:30:00,095
in any kind of research
because you never really know

621
00:30:00,095 --> 00:30:02,470
if the systems that you're
developing or the methods that

622
00:30:02,470 --> 00:30:05,670
you're developing are going
to improve over some existing

623
00:30:05,670 --> 00:30:06,925
state-of-the-art method.

624
00:30:06,925 --> 00:30:08,550
And if you're very
honest with yourself

625
00:30:08,550 --> 00:30:13,270
about what might go wrong
and why might not actually

626
00:30:13,270 --> 00:30:15,990
see benefits from your
new idea, then you

627
00:30:15,990 --> 00:30:18,310
can think more critically
about what sorts of things

628
00:30:18,310 --> 00:30:22,410
will actually improve
the final results.

629
00:30:22,410 --> 00:30:24,410
Cool.

630
00:30:24,410 --> 00:30:25,750
So supervising each level.

631
00:30:25,750 --> 00:30:28,010
So we have the low-level policy.

632
00:30:28,010 --> 00:30:30,210
And the low-level
policy will be trained

633
00:30:30,210 --> 00:30:34,610
to accomplish that goal,
not the original task.

634
00:30:34,610 --> 00:30:38,710
And so when you're passing
in the string of "find pot,"

635
00:30:38,710 --> 00:30:42,090
it's going to be getting
a reward that is trying

636
00:30:42,090 --> 00:30:46,370
to maximize reward for getting
to the states where you found

637
00:30:46,370 --> 00:30:48,250
the pot.

638
00:30:48,250 --> 00:30:51,050
And the high-level policy--

639
00:30:51,050 --> 00:30:54,090
its goal is to
predict the subtasks

640
00:30:54,090 --> 00:31:01,490
so that you end up in the final
state of the pasta being cooked.

641
00:31:01,490 --> 00:31:05,810
So this is kind of trying to
accomplish the original goal.

642
00:31:05,810 --> 00:31:08,290
Now there's a couple
of things that can be

643
00:31:08,290 --> 00:31:10,390
a little bit tricky about this.

644
00:31:10,390 --> 00:31:13,690
So say that you're
training these separately

645
00:31:13,690 --> 00:31:17,890
and say that you've trained--

646
00:31:17,890 --> 00:31:20,290
you could, for example,
think about training a policy

647
00:31:20,290 --> 00:31:22,860
to find the pot,
and maybe you've

648
00:31:22,860 --> 00:31:26,372
gotten to a good policy that's
really good at finding the pot

649
00:31:26,372 --> 00:31:28,080
and getting to the
state really reliably.

650
00:31:28,080 --> 00:31:33,820

651
00:31:33,820 --> 00:31:37,580
And then maybe for example,
when you train the policy

652
00:31:37,580 --> 00:31:38,630
for finding the pasta.

653
00:31:38,630 --> 00:31:43,152

654
00:31:43,152 --> 00:31:45,360
If you weren't exactly
careful with how you did this,

655
00:31:45,360 --> 00:31:47,860
maybe you would think about,
OK, maybe I could actually just

656
00:31:47,860 --> 00:31:49,420
start from this
state and also try

657
00:31:49,420 --> 00:31:53,452
to find the pasta from
this state over here.

658
00:31:53,452 --> 00:31:55,660
And that's not taking into
account that actually when

659
00:31:55,660 --> 00:31:57,260
it's finding the
pasta, according

660
00:31:57,260 --> 00:31:59,900
to the high-level policy, if
the high-level policy actually

661
00:31:59,900 --> 00:32:03,860
wanted it to find the pasta
after boiling the water when

662
00:32:03,860 --> 00:32:05,540
you're at the stove.

663
00:32:05,540 --> 00:32:07,620
Then when you actually
try to string together

664
00:32:07,620 --> 00:32:12,300
this low-level policy with the
low-level policy for these two.

665
00:32:12,300 --> 00:32:14,100
It'll actually then
struggle to transition

666
00:32:14,100 --> 00:32:19,100
from going to the stove over
to finding where the pasta is.

667
00:32:19,100 --> 00:32:21,950
And this is basically like
a mismatch when you're

668
00:32:21,950 --> 00:32:24,807
training these modular systems.

669
00:32:24,807 --> 00:32:26,890
And you're training them
completely independently.

670
00:32:26,890 --> 00:32:30,270
You might not actually get a
system that works effectively

671
00:32:30,270 --> 00:32:33,390
if they're not actually at
some point connected together

672
00:32:33,390 --> 00:32:38,322
and trained such that this
skill is starting from states

673
00:32:38,322 --> 00:32:40,030
where the high-level
policy will actually

674
00:32:40,030 --> 00:32:42,950
start commanding that task.

675
00:32:42,950 --> 00:32:47,990
And so essentially when you're
training the low-level policy--

676
00:32:47,990 --> 00:32:52,350
so you have your
low-level policy actions,

677
00:32:52,350 --> 00:32:54,270
given states and goals.

678
00:32:54,270 --> 00:32:58,230
You want this policy to
work for the set of states

679
00:32:58,230 --> 00:33:00,990
where the high-level
policy is going to command

680
00:33:00,990 --> 00:33:03,630
this particular goal.

681
00:33:03,630 --> 00:33:07,262
Another way to think
about this is that--

682
00:33:07,262 --> 00:33:08,940
and I guess the other
thing here is also

683
00:33:08,940 --> 00:33:11,190
you want it to be able to
pick the goals that actually

684
00:33:11,190 --> 00:33:13,410
the high-level
policy would predict.

685
00:33:13,410 --> 00:33:17,350
So for different
distributions of goals

686
00:33:17,350 --> 00:33:20,370
and different distributions
of states, ideally,

687
00:33:20,370 --> 00:33:22,610
that low level policy
would work for whatever

688
00:33:22,610 --> 00:33:25,820
states and goals the
high-level policy will output.

689
00:33:25,820 --> 00:33:28,170

690
00:33:28,170 --> 00:33:30,670
Now, if we haven't actually
learned a high-level policy yet,

691
00:33:30,670 --> 00:33:35,090
this puts things in a bit
of a tricky situation.

692
00:33:35,090 --> 00:33:37,930
And likewise for the
high-level policy,

693
00:33:37,930 --> 00:33:42,450
if we assume that our low-level
policy is really great,

694
00:33:42,450 --> 00:33:47,210
then we might not
realize that it actually

695
00:33:47,210 --> 00:33:49,710
has some deficiencies and
that we need to, for example,

696
00:33:49,710 --> 00:33:52,650
continue to command
something in certain states

697
00:33:52,650 --> 00:33:55,530
until you've actually reached a
state that a different skill can

698
00:33:55,530 --> 00:33:57,130
operate in.

699
00:33:57,130 --> 00:33:58,090
Yeah.

700
00:33:58,090 --> 00:34:00,748
So if the low-level policy
works from particular sequences

701
00:34:00,748 --> 00:34:02,290
of states, then the
high-level policy

702
00:34:02,290 --> 00:34:05,490
can learn that it should
walk the low-level policy

703
00:34:05,490 --> 00:34:06,510
through those states.

704
00:34:06,510 --> 00:34:08,668
So you don't necessarily
need your low-level policy

705
00:34:08,668 --> 00:34:09,710
to work from every state.

706
00:34:09,710 --> 00:34:12,130
It just needs to work for
the state-goal combinations

707
00:34:12,130 --> 00:34:14,449
that the high-level policy
is going to command.

708
00:34:14,449 --> 00:34:17,580
Now, you might have
a situation where,

709
00:34:17,580 --> 00:34:21,860
say, that your
low-level policy knows

710
00:34:21,860 --> 00:34:23,920
how to do something like this.

711
00:34:23,920 --> 00:34:26,040
So it knows how to
go from here to here.

712
00:34:26,040 --> 00:34:27,540
It knows how to go
from here to here

713
00:34:27,540 --> 00:34:31,739
and maybe knows how to
go from here to here,

714
00:34:31,739 --> 00:34:33,340
like with some boiled water.

715
00:34:33,340 --> 00:34:34,940
Then you might have
some sort of gap

716
00:34:34,940 --> 00:34:36,920
where to actually
find the pasta,

717
00:34:36,920 --> 00:34:39,100
you need to go back
to these states.

718
00:34:39,100 --> 00:34:42,699
And if you don't have
a command or some way

719
00:34:42,699 --> 00:34:45,100
to get from here to
here, then you're

720
00:34:45,100 --> 00:34:47,281
going to basically have
a gap in the states

721
00:34:47,281 --> 00:34:48,239
that you're visited in.

722
00:34:48,239 --> 00:34:50,420
The high-level policy
will struggle to actually

723
00:34:50,420 --> 00:34:52,300
get to this state here.

724
00:34:52,300 --> 00:34:53,820
And so basically,
one way to think

725
00:34:53,820 --> 00:34:56,580
about it is you need to make
sure that there's at least some

726
00:34:56,580 --> 00:35:00,500
overlap in the states from
one skill to the next.

727
00:35:00,500 --> 00:35:04,300
And ideally, that
overlap has some buffer.

728
00:35:04,300 --> 00:35:08,340
Because if, for example, when
you're trying to find the pasta,

729
00:35:08,340 --> 00:35:10,660
it only works if
you're standing exactly

730
00:35:10,660 --> 00:35:12,660
in front of the boiled
water and not if you're

731
00:35:12,660 --> 00:35:14,140
like a little bit to the right.

732
00:35:14,140 --> 00:35:17,152
Then if your policy ends up
deciding to boil the water,

733
00:35:17,152 --> 00:35:18,860
standing a little bit
to the right of it,

734
00:35:18,860 --> 00:35:22,640
then you'll get a brittle
final result where

735
00:35:22,640 --> 00:35:24,700
it will struggle in that case.

736
00:35:24,700 --> 00:35:30,320

737
00:35:30,320 --> 00:35:32,080
I guess one way
to think about it

738
00:35:32,080 --> 00:35:34,880
is that if this
low-level policy only

739
00:35:34,880 --> 00:35:39,640
works from this
part of the states

740
00:35:39,640 --> 00:35:42,800
where the water is being boiled
and the high-level policy gets

741
00:35:42,800 --> 00:35:45,500
you here, or the previous
low-level policy gets you here,

742
00:35:45,500 --> 00:35:48,000
then you're going to have a
little bit of a gap between here

743
00:35:48,000 --> 00:35:48,680
and here.

744
00:35:48,680 --> 00:35:51,040
And that sort of gap can
lead to brittle performance

745
00:35:51,040 --> 00:35:53,580
on the full long-horizon task.

746
00:35:53,580 --> 00:35:54,080
Yeah.

747
00:35:54,080 --> 00:35:56,460
So the question is, does it
have to be a fixed sequence?

748
00:35:56,460 --> 00:35:58,520
And can you learn
if one sequence

749
00:35:58,520 --> 00:36:00,920
is more efficient than another?

750
00:36:00,920 --> 00:36:01,420
Yeah.

751
00:36:01,420 --> 00:36:03,160
So absolutely, the
high-level policy

752
00:36:03,160 --> 00:36:05,280
can actually decide which
sequence it wants to do,

753
00:36:05,280 --> 00:36:07,780
and that sequence might depend
on what's the most efficient.

754
00:36:07,780 --> 00:36:13,920
It might also depend on
where the low-level policy is

755
00:36:13,920 --> 00:36:14,990
most successful.

756
00:36:14,990 --> 00:36:18,010
And so one of the reasons why
I wanted to pick this example

757
00:36:18,010 --> 00:36:20,830
is actually these two steps
can be done in any order.

758
00:36:20,830 --> 00:36:23,470
And the high-level policy could
decide that when you're here,

759
00:36:23,470 --> 00:36:25,730
it actually wants to
command you to go here.

760
00:36:25,730 --> 00:36:27,250
And as long as the
low-level policy

761
00:36:27,250 --> 00:36:29,970
knows how to find the
pasta from these states,

762
00:36:29,970 --> 00:36:32,900
then that might be
more efficient overall.

763
00:36:32,900 --> 00:36:34,650
There's also worlds
where maybe this would

764
00:36:34,650 --> 00:36:36,510
be more efficient because maybe
while you're boiling the water,

765
00:36:36,510 --> 00:36:37,343
you're just waiting.

766
00:36:37,343 --> 00:36:41,147
And you can find the
pasta in the meantime.

767
00:36:41,147 --> 00:36:43,730
And so I guess there's also a
pouring water that-- and turning

768
00:36:43,730 --> 00:36:44,813
on the stove and so forth.

769
00:36:44,813 --> 00:36:46,150
So there's more steps involved.

770
00:36:46,150 --> 00:36:48,090
But yeah, the high-level
policy can decide

771
00:36:48,090 --> 00:36:52,230
which order it wants to take.

772
00:36:52,230 --> 00:36:55,450
So generally the
way to address this

773
00:36:55,450 --> 00:36:57,970
is that you can first train
them potentially completely

774
00:36:57,970 --> 00:36:59,130
separately.

775
00:36:59,130 --> 00:37:01,730
But it's important for
at least one policy

776
00:37:01,730 --> 00:37:05,450
to be adapted to the other.

777
00:37:05,450 --> 00:37:10,810
And ideally, even both could be
jointly adapted with each other

778
00:37:10,810 --> 00:37:18,590
to optimize reward, or optimize
some complete objective.

779
00:37:18,590 --> 00:37:19,590
Cool.

780
00:37:19,590 --> 00:37:22,750
One other side note,
I guess, that I

781
00:37:22,750 --> 00:37:26,134
think is worth
mentioning is that the--

782
00:37:26,134 --> 00:37:31,430
even for problems that are not
traditionally LLM problems--

783
00:37:31,430 --> 00:37:34,110
LLMs are actually often
pretty good at some

784
00:37:34,110 --> 00:37:37,430
of the higher-level reasoning
that you might want to do.

785
00:37:37,430 --> 00:37:40,538
And so, for example, if you know
that you want to cook pasta,

786
00:37:40,538 --> 00:37:42,830
if you ask a language model
what are the steps involved

787
00:37:42,830 --> 00:37:45,790
with that, oftentimes, they
can actually give you that.

788
00:37:45,790 --> 00:37:48,950
And so this is one way
where you can actually

789
00:37:48,950 --> 00:37:51,470
train the high-level policy
completely separately

790
00:37:51,470 --> 00:37:54,703
from the low-level policy and
then potentially fine-tune them

791
00:37:54,703 --> 00:37:56,370
because the language
model, for example,

792
00:37:56,370 --> 00:38:02,590
might not know which order the
low-level policy is best suited

793
00:38:02,590 --> 00:38:06,590
for, or other things
about the capabilities

794
00:38:06,590 --> 00:38:09,225
of the low-level policy.

795
00:38:09,225 --> 00:38:10,850
And so that sort of
adaptation process,

796
00:38:10,850 --> 00:38:12,380
even though language models
are pretty good at this,

797
00:38:12,380 --> 00:38:15,000
that sort of adaptation
process is really important.

798
00:38:15,000 --> 00:38:18,280
Also, if a robot is in
front of a coffee machine

799
00:38:18,280 --> 00:38:20,513
and you ask the robot to
get some coffee for you,

800
00:38:20,513 --> 00:38:22,680
if the language model doesn't
actually pay attention

801
00:38:22,680 --> 00:38:25,860
to that image, it might
tell the robot like, oh,

802
00:38:25,860 --> 00:38:29,492
can you go out and buy coffee
at Starbucks or something?

803
00:38:29,492 --> 00:38:31,700
And the robot might not have
that sort of affordance,

804
00:38:31,700 --> 00:38:33,360
that sort of ability to do that.

805
00:38:33,360 --> 00:38:35,322
And so adapting
these two together,

806
00:38:35,322 --> 00:38:37,280
that's another example
where adapting these two

807
00:38:37,280 --> 00:38:38,655
high-level and
low-level policies

808
00:38:38,655 --> 00:38:40,260
together is quite important.

809
00:38:40,260 --> 00:38:43,376

810
00:38:43,376 --> 00:38:47,920
The last key design choice that
we haven't talked about yet

811
00:38:47,920 --> 00:38:51,280
is when to move from
one task to another.

812
00:38:51,280 --> 00:38:55,240
So we outlined
this process here,

813
00:38:55,240 --> 00:38:57,860
and we didn't really spend
that much time thinking about

814
00:38:57,860 --> 00:39:01,200
like, how long do
you repeat sampling

815
00:39:01,200 --> 00:39:04,760
from your low-level policy
to accomplish a goal?

816
00:39:04,760 --> 00:39:06,760
And essentially,
it's how often do we

817
00:39:06,760 --> 00:39:09,560
requery a new goal from
the high-level policy

818
00:39:09,560 --> 00:39:13,290
to pass through the
low-level policy.

819
00:39:13,290 --> 00:39:18,170
So basically, yeah,
this step right here.

820
00:39:18,170 --> 00:39:24,330
So there's two natural
options for this.

821
00:39:24,330 --> 00:39:29,730
The first natural option is to
requery the high-level policy

822
00:39:29,730 --> 00:39:33,110
whenever the low-level policy
has completed the goal.

823
00:39:33,110 --> 00:39:35,810
So when it has boiled
the water, that's

824
00:39:35,810 --> 00:39:40,890
when you tell it to go find
the pasta, for example.

825
00:39:40,890 --> 00:39:43,090
And the way that this
would look like is--

826
00:39:43,090 --> 00:39:45,210
at this point, your
low-level policy not only

827
00:39:45,210 --> 00:39:49,290
needs to predict action but
also needs to predict whether

828
00:39:49,290 --> 00:39:53,610
or it's successfully
completed the task.

829
00:39:53,610 --> 00:39:57,290
And this could be either with
some sort of binary indicator

830
00:39:57,290 --> 00:40:01,050
or through some sort
of progress indicator.

831
00:40:01,050 --> 00:40:03,490
You can think about this
not done as a success

832
00:40:03,490 --> 00:40:05,580
classifier for the task.

833
00:40:05,580 --> 00:40:08,330

834
00:40:08,330 --> 00:40:14,390
And then the other option that's
a bit simpler but maybe less

835
00:40:14,390 --> 00:40:17,550
elegant, in some ways,
is just to simply requery

836
00:40:17,550 --> 00:40:21,230
the high-level policy after
some fixed number of timesteps.

837
00:40:21,230 --> 00:40:27,490
So maybe you query the
low-level policy at 50 hertz,

838
00:40:27,490 --> 00:40:31,590
and you query the high-level
policy once every second.

839
00:40:31,590 --> 00:40:34,350
So then you would be querying
the high-level policy

840
00:40:34,350 --> 00:40:35,770
after 50 timesteps.

841
00:40:35,770 --> 00:40:38,430

842
00:40:38,430 --> 00:40:42,710
So there's pros and cons of
each of these two options.

843
00:40:42,710 --> 00:40:45,590
For the first case where
we're doing something

844
00:40:45,590 --> 00:40:48,855
after we've completed a
goal, this is actually ideal.

845
00:40:48,855 --> 00:40:50,230
If you were to do
this well, this

846
00:40:50,230 --> 00:40:52,390
should be the most
compute efficient

847
00:40:52,390 --> 00:40:55,070
because you're waiting
for the low-level policy.

848
00:40:55,070 --> 00:40:56,910
You don't need to
rethink how you're

849
00:40:56,910 --> 00:41:00,010
doing the task while the
low-level policy is executing.

850
00:41:00,010 --> 00:41:02,488

851
00:41:02,488 --> 00:41:05,030
There's a couple of things that
are tricky about this though.

852
00:41:05,030 --> 00:41:06,670
The first is that
it can actually

853
00:41:06,670 --> 00:41:11,400
be hard to estimate when
you're done with a task.

854
00:41:11,400 --> 00:41:14,702
It's definitely not
trivial to do this.

855
00:41:14,702 --> 00:41:16,160
And the second is
that you actually

856
00:41:16,160 --> 00:41:20,000
need to account for mistakes
that require actually

857
00:41:20,000 --> 00:41:22,560
redoing some past goal.

858
00:41:22,560 --> 00:41:26,080
So for example,
if you are trying

859
00:41:26,080 --> 00:41:29,320
to organize some things on a
table, you go grab something,

860
00:41:29,320 --> 00:41:31,600
and then you accidentally
drop it on the ground.

861
00:41:31,600 --> 00:41:34,160
Your subgoal should no longer
be like, put that object

862
00:41:34,160 --> 00:41:36,080
on the table, and
instead it should be

863
00:41:36,080 --> 00:41:38,098
to pick it up off the ground.

864
00:41:38,098 --> 00:41:39,640
And this means that
actually you need

865
00:41:39,640 --> 00:41:42,040
to now estimate not
just when you're

866
00:41:42,040 --> 00:41:47,800
done but also whether
or not you've actually

867
00:41:47,800 --> 00:41:51,490
exited the states in which
that subtask makes sense.

868
00:41:51,490 --> 00:41:55,240

869
00:41:55,240 --> 00:41:59,240
Then going back to the
fixed number of timesteps,

870
00:41:59,240 --> 00:42:03,160
this is less ideal because we're
going to be probably wasting

871
00:42:03,160 --> 00:42:05,480
some compute, and
there's going to be

872
00:42:05,480 --> 00:42:08,120
a trade-off between
spending more compute,

873
00:42:08,120 --> 00:42:11,860
thinking through what
the next goal should be,

874
00:42:11,860 --> 00:42:14,380
versus if this n is too long.

875
00:42:14,380 --> 00:42:16,820
Then you're going to be
delayed in transitioning

876
00:42:16,820 --> 00:42:20,620
to the new goal, where your
agent will be-- for example,

877
00:42:20,620 --> 00:42:22,480
maybe it's found the pot.

878
00:42:22,480 --> 00:42:24,965
It's taken out the pot, and
it's waiting for a new goal.

879
00:42:24,965 --> 00:42:26,340
If n is too long,
it's just going

880
00:42:26,340 --> 00:42:28,632
to be sitting there with the
pot because it's like, OK,

881
00:42:28,632 --> 00:42:29,460
I found the pot.

882
00:42:29,460 --> 00:42:31,072
And that's the goal
that it's getting.

883
00:42:31,072 --> 00:42:32,780
And there'll be some
delay before it then

884
00:42:32,780 --> 00:42:34,700
moves on to actually
finding the pasta

885
00:42:34,700 --> 00:42:37,220
or pouring water into the pot.

886
00:42:37,220 --> 00:42:40,780
So this trade-off between
how long n should be.

887
00:42:40,780 --> 00:42:44,320
Generally, because you don't
want these really long delays,

888
00:42:44,320 --> 00:42:49,660
you'll pick n to be fairly
small or fairly frequent.

889
00:42:49,660 --> 00:42:52,140
But when n is
smaller, you're then

890
00:42:52,140 --> 00:42:55,060
going to be placing more burden
on your high-level policy

891
00:42:55,060 --> 00:42:59,020
because it needs to be making
accurate goal predictions more

892
00:42:59,020 --> 00:43:02,220
frequently on more states.

893
00:43:02,220 --> 00:43:06,510
And you'll be spending
more compute as well.

894
00:43:06,510 --> 00:43:08,120
So both of these
have pros and cons.

895
00:43:08,120 --> 00:43:10,870

896
00:43:10,870 --> 00:43:17,670
In general, if we wanted to
think about how these pros

897
00:43:17,670 --> 00:43:19,190
and cons relate
to each other, we

898
00:43:19,190 --> 00:43:21,230
can think about how
these pros and cons lead

899
00:43:21,230 --> 00:43:24,070
to different
downstream performance.

900
00:43:24,070 --> 00:43:28,110
And with regard to
estimating when you're done

901
00:43:28,110 --> 00:43:30,230
and accounting for
mistakes, if you actually

902
00:43:30,230 --> 00:43:33,843
make a mistake in estimating
when you're done, then

903
00:43:33,843 --> 00:43:35,510
you could actually
end up in a situation

904
00:43:35,510 --> 00:43:39,142
where the agent is
perpetually stuck.

905
00:43:39,142 --> 00:43:41,950
If it thinks that it's not
done but it is actually done,

906
00:43:41,950 --> 00:43:44,510
then it will actually
just stay in that state

907
00:43:44,510 --> 00:43:48,110
because the low-level policy
will keep on predicting this--

908
00:43:48,110 --> 00:43:50,430
won't actually be making
any more further progress

909
00:43:50,430 --> 00:43:52,550
on the task.

910
00:43:52,550 --> 00:43:56,710
So this actually is a
pretty rough outcome

911
00:43:56,710 --> 00:44:01,470
for these kinds of errors,
even if they're infrequent.

912
00:44:01,470 --> 00:44:04,530
And then for option two,
if you make a mistake

913
00:44:04,530 --> 00:44:06,370
in predicting the
subtask because you're

914
00:44:06,370 --> 00:44:08,890
querying the high-level
policy more frequently,

915
00:44:08,890 --> 00:44:11,490
this also has bad outcome, which
is that the low-level policy

916
00:44:11,490 --> 00:44:14,830
might stop making
progress on the final task

917
00:44:14,830 --> 00:44:17,970
and might be taking the
wrong actions for n steps

918
00:44:17,970 --> 00:44:22,650
until you get hopefully a
more accurate goal prediction.

919
00:44:22,650 --> 00:44:24,850
And generally, between
these two things,

920
00:44:24,850 --> 00:44:28,130
these errors are more fatal.

921
00:44:28,130 --> 00:44:32,610
And as a result, typically, a
lot of the hierarchical systems

922
00:44:32,610 --> 00:44:36,010
out there opt for choosing
option two because

923
00:44:36,010 --> 00:44:38,322
of the simplicity
and because some

924
00:44:38,322 --> 00:44:40,530
of the mistakes that the
high-level policy might make

925
00:44:40,530 --> 00:44:42,170
when it's queried
more frequently

926
00:44:42,170 --> 00:44:44,450
are less fatal than
mistakes with regard

927
00:44:44,450 --> 00:44:47,650
to estimating when you're
done and at what subtask,

928
00:44:47,650 --> 00:44:50,242
whether or not you should
re-account for past goals.

929
00:44:50,242 --> 00:44:52,450
It's also worth mentioning
that you could potentially

930
00:44:52,450 --> 00:44:55,350
combine these, where you
try to estimate progress.

931
00:44:55,350 --> 00:44:57,430
But then if it's been
a long period of time,

932
00:44:57,430 --> 00:44:59,610
then you're re-query anyway.

933
00:44:59,610 --> 00:45:02,010
But yeah, the takeaway is
that, generally, option two

934
00:45:02,010 --> 00:45:04,500
is typically what people
do, where you just query

935
00:45:04,500 --> 00:45:07,860
at a fixed lower frequency.

936
00:45:07,860 --> 00:45:08,760
Cool.

937
00:45:08,760 --> 00:45:11,500
So let's look at
some example systems.

938
00:45:11,500 --> 00:45:14,140
And I guess one thing
that I'll mention

939
00:45:14,140 --> 00:45:17,320
is that hierarchy is
pretty popular these days.

940
00:45:17,320 --> 00:45:23,660
So here are four
systems that we're all

941
00:45:23,660 --> 00:45:27,340
announced in the last
five months, four months.

942
00:45:27,340 --> 00:45:29,560
And they actually all
use hierarchical systems.

943
00:45:29,560 --> 00:45:31,620
So these are all fairly
long horizon tasks,

944
00:45:31,620 --> 00:45:35,060
where you're cleaning a
bedroom, handing over objects,

945
00:45:35,060 --> 00:45:37,860
and pouring them or
putting them into bins,

946
00:45:37,860 --> 00:45:40,320
putting away objects
into a fridge,

947
00:45:40,320 --> 00:45:43,300
putting fruit into bowls.

948
00:45:43,300 --> 00:45:46,100
And if you look at the
architectures for these things,

949
00:45:46,100 --> 00:45:50,220
they all have some
higher-level model

950
00:45:50,220 --> 00:45:52,020
that's predicting
intermediate goals

951
00:45:52,020 --> 00:45:57,740
and some lower-level model that
is predicting low-level actions.

952
00:45:57,740 --> 00:45:59,120
These are in robotics domains.

953
00:45:59,120 --> 00:46:01,753
Likewise in language
model domains,

954
00:46:01,753 --> 00:46:03,920
things like chain of thought,
where you're reasoning

955
00:46:03,920 --> 00:46:05,120
through intermediate steps.

956
00:46:05,120 --> 00:46:07,680
And then actually outputting
tokens for those steps

957
00:46:07,680 --> 00:46:12,400
is also really popular for
completing more challenging

958
00:46:12,400 --> 00:46:13,190
language tasks.

959
00:46:13,190 --> 00:46:16,480

960
00:46:16,480 --> 00:46:17,580
Cool.

961
00:46:17,580 --> 00:46:20,720
So I want to cover a few
examples of these systems,

962
00:46:20,720 --> 00:46:23,560
and I picked
examples that I think

963
00:46:23,560 --> 00:46:26,440
illustrate a few different
design choices that are helpful

964
00:46:26,440 --> 00:46:29,360
and actually have comparisons
on those design choices

965
00:46:29,360 --> 00:46:32,400
that elucidate some of
the benefits of one design

966
00:46:32,400 --> 00:46:34,787
choice versus another.

967
00:46:34,787 --> 00:46:37,120
So let's start with talking
about hierarchical imitation

968
00:46:37,120 --> 00:46:38,945
learning systems.

969
00:46:38,945 --> 00:46:40,570
And we'll start with
language subgoals.

970
00:46:40,570 --> 00:46:45,275

971
00:46:45,275 --> 00:46:47,400
And let's first talk about
what the data would look

972
00:46:47,400 --> 00:46:49,600
like for training a hierarchical
imitation learning system

973
00:46:49,600 --> 00:46:50,558
with language subgoals.

974
00:46:50,558 --> 00:46:55,160

975
00:46:55,160 --> 00:46:58,146
This will be kind of
a real robot example.

976
00:46:58,146 --> 00:46:59,720
What the data looks
like is actually

977
00:46:59,720 --> 00:47:03,650
it's going to use data of a
long-horizon demonstration

978
00:47:03,650 --> 00:47:07,010
of completing the complete
long-horizon task.

979
00:47:07,010 --> 00:47:09,610
And so in this case, the
task is to make trail mix

980
00:47:09,610 --> 00:47:12,210
and put the trail
mix into a bag.

981
00:47:12,210 --> 00:47:16,770
And with this long-horizon
demonstration for the full task,

982
00:47:16,770 --> 00:47:20,890
it will then be segmented
with language labels

983
00:47:20,890 --> 00:47:22,890
for each segment.

984
00:47:22,890 --> 00:47:26,490
And in this case, the
segments in the data

985
00:47:26,490 --> 00:47:28,470
can have a variable length.

986
00:47:28,470 --> 00:47:32,690

987
00:47:32,690 --> 00:47:37,450
If, for example,
the robot trajectory

988
00:47:37,450 --> 00:47:39,590
looks something like this,
then you might have--

989
00:47:39,590 --> 00:47:42,270
the first subtask is
something like this.

990
00:47:42,270 --> 00:47:44,450
The second subtask
is maybe shorter.

991
00:47:44,450 --> 00:47:46,430
The third subtask
is maybe longer.

992
00:47:46,430 --> 00:47:49,250
So you'll have variable
frequency in the data,

993
00:47:49,250 --> 00:47:53,450
but the actual system will later
be using a fixed frequency where

994
00:47:53,450 --> 00:47:59,340
it's requiring more frequently
than the typical segment

995
00:47:59,340 --> 00:48:00,320
length in the data.

996
00:48:00,320 --> 00:48:03,020

997
00:48:03,020 --> 00:48:05,100
So these are different
language labels.

998
00:48:05,100 --> 00:48:10,403
These labels could either be
recorded during data collection,

999
00:48:10,403 --> 00:48:12,820
where you're actually saying,
I'm going to pick up the bag

1000
00:48:12,820 --> 00:48:14,720
and then do a demonstration
of picking up the bag.

1001
00:48:14,720 --> 00:48:16,678
Then say, I'm going to
pick up the metal scoop,

1002
00:48:16,678 --> 00:48:18,993
do a demonstration
of that in sequence,

1003
00:48:18,993 --> 00:48:20,660
or you can do it
post-hoc where you just

1004
00:48:20,660 --> 00:48:22,480
collect the full demonstration.

1005
00:48:22,480 --> 00:48:25,060
And then with a
labeling interface label

1006
00:48:25,060 --> 00:48:26,808
like, this segment is
picking up the bag.

1007
00:48:26,808 --> 00:48:28,600
This segment is picking
up the metal scoop.

1008
00:48:28,600 --> 00:48:33,602
This segment is getting
M&Ms and so forth.

1009
00:48:33,602 --> 00:48:35,060
So we have segmented
demonstrations

1010
00:48:35,060 --> 00:48:37,220
with the associated
language command.

1011
00:48:37,220 --> 00:48:40,140
Here's another example of
what this would look like.

1012
00:48:40,140 --> 00:48:43,300
And this shows that one segment
might be longer for picking up

1013
00:48:43,300 --> 00:48:45,380
a slice of cheese,
a different segment

1014
00:48:45,380 --> 00:48:47,660
might be shorter for putting
the cheese on the bread

1015
00:48:47,660 --> 00:48:48,290
and so forth.

1016
00:48:48,290 --> 00:48:51,360

1017
00:48:51,360 --> 00:48:51,860
Cool.

1018
00:48:51,860 --> 00:48:54,460
And then once we
have this data, we'll

1019
00:48:54,460 --> 00:48:57,020
train a high-level policy
and a low-level policy

1020
00:48:57,020 --> 00:48:58,640
with imitation learning.

1021
00:48:58,640 --> 00:49:01,640
And the high-level policy will
be outputting the language

1022
00:49:01,640 --> 00:49:04,600
labels from the observations.

1023
00:49:04,600 --> 00:49:08,400
And the low level will
be taking the language

1024
00:49:08,400 --> 00:49:13,320
labels and the observations
and predicting the actions.

1025
00:49:13,320 --> 00:49:17,940
So what this looks like
is an image observation.

1026
00:49:17,940 --> 00:49:20,840
So an RGB image passes it
into a high-level policy

1027
00:49:20,840 --> 00:49:24,360
that's trained to predict
the language label.

1028
00:49:24,360 --> 00:49:27,860
And then you'll have a low-level
policy that takes the language,

1029
00:49:27,860 --> 00:49:30,140
in addition to the observations.

1030
00:49:30,140 --> 00:49:34,780
In this case, the work chooses
to use not just RGB images

1031
00:49:34,780 --> 00:49:38,280
but also joint angles as
input to the low-level policy.

1032
00:49:38,280 --> 00:49:40,320
And generally, it's
OK to have actually

1033
00:49:40,320 --> 00:49:43,120
this sort of mismatch between
observations for the high-level

1034
00:49:43,120 --> 00:49:45,040
and the low-level policy.

1035
00:49:45,040 --> 00:49:49,840
And then the low-level policy
outputs joint commands.

1036
00:49:49,840 --> 00:49:52,840
And the low-level policy
operates at 50 hertz.

1037
00:49:52,840 --> 00:49:54,520
And I can't remember
the exact frequency

1038
00:49:54,520 --> 00:49:56,062
of the high-level
policy, but I think

1039
00:49:56,062 --> 00:50:00,310
it's something around 1 hertz.

1040
00:50:00,310 --> 00:50:03,130

1041
00:50:03,130 --> 00:50:05,110
Cool.

1042
00:50:05,110 --> 00:50:07,130
So that's the general setup.

1043
00:50:07,130 --> 00:50:09,850
Now we also learned
about DAgger.

1044
00:50:09,850 --> 00:50:13,090
And you can think
about if we just

1045
00:50:13,090 --> 00:50:15,370
do imitation learning
on the offline data,

1046
00:50:15,370 --> 00:50:18,210
we might have issues
where we're training these

1047
00:50:18,210 --> 00:50:20,810
somewhat independently,
although the data is at least

1048
00:50:20,810 --> 00:50:23,810
of the full long-horizon task.

1049
00:50:23,810 --> 00:50:29,610
And we could actually use
DAgger with these systems

1050
00:50:29,610 --> 00:50:33,555
on both the low-level policy
and the high-level policy.

1051
00:50:33,555 --> 00:50:34,930
Now, one thing
that's pretty cool

1052
00:50:34,930 --> 00:50:36,722
is that actually for
the high-level policy,

1053
00:50:36,722 --> 00:50:38,770
we can actually update
the high-level policy

1054
00:50:38,770 --> 00:50:40,850
with language supervision.

1055
00:50:40,850 --> 00:50:43,090
And so DAgger on the
high-level policy

1056
00:50:43,090 --> 00:50:48,930
is actually in language
space, not in joint space.

1057
00:50:48,930 --> 00:50:55,310
And so in principle, you can
do DAgger on both low level

1058
00:50:55,310 --> 00:50:55,972
and high level.

1059
00:50:55,972 --> 00:50:57,430
In this particular
approach, if you

1060
00:50:57,430 --> 00:51:00,150
were to do it on the high level
with freezing the low level,

1061
00:51:00,150 --> 00:51:05,430
you would be running the
policy, the full system.

1062
00:51:05,430 --> 00:51:09,110
And then you would
then type or speak

1063
00:51:09,110 --> 00:51:11,670
a language command that
is going to override

1064
00:51:11,670 --> 00:51:17,745
the high-level policy and
then continue collecting data.

1065
00:51:17,745 --> 00:51:19,870
And then at some point,
you might give control back

1066
00:51:19,870 --> 00:51:23,065
to the high-level policy.

1067
00:51:23,065 --> 00:51:25,190
And then from there, you
can fine-tune and actually

1068
00:51:25,190 --> 00:51:28,510
do gradient steps on data that
has these kinds of language

1069
00:51:28,510 --> 00:51:32,270
interventions to actually
fine-tune the high-level policy.

1070
00:51:32,270 --> 00:51:35,870
And when you do this together
with the high-level policy

1071
00:51:35,870 --> 00:51:40,170
and the low-level policy
acting as one system,

1072
00:51:40,170 --> 00:51:42,470
then you can actually
adapt the high-level policy

1073
00:51:42,470 --> 00:51:44,990
to deficiencies in
the low-level policy.

1074
00:51:44,990 --> 00:51:46,990
So, for example, if there's
one language command

1075
00:51:46,990 --> 00:51:50,150
that the low-level policy
makes a mistake on,

1076
00:51:50,150 --> 00:51:54,796
then you could actually issue
some corrections to help

1077
00:51:54,796 --> 00:51:56,730
compensate for that mistake.

1078
00:51:56,730 --> 00:52:00,560

1079
00:52:00,560 --> 00:52:01,220
So yeah.

1080
00:52:01,220 --> 00:52:04,240
I guess to summarize, we can
train the high-level policy

1081
00:52:04,240 --> 00:52:06,440
and the low-level policy,
both with imitation,

1082
00:52:06,440 --> 00:52:08,320
and then freeze the
low-level policy

1083
00:52:08,320 --> 00:52:11,320
and update the high level policy
with DAgger by supervising

1084
00:52:11,320 --> 00:52:15,080
on language corrections.

1085
00:52:15,080 --> 00:52:17,280
And so as a video of
what this looks like,

1086
00:52:17,280 --> 00:52:21,000
these yellow commands
are showing the output

1087
00:52:21,000 --> 00:52:22,920
of the high-level policy.

1088
00:52:22,920 --> 00:52:25,050
And then the robot motions
are showing the output

1089
00:52:25,050 --> 00:52:26,050
of the low-level policy.

1090
00:52:26,050 --> 00:52:30,040

1091
00:52:30,040 --> 00:52:33,360
So at the beginning, the
high-level policy is saying,

1092
00:52:33,360 --> 00:52:34,020
pick up spoon.

1093
00:52:34,020 --> 00:52:36,560
The robot picks up the spoon,
move the spoon towards me.

1094
00:52:36,560 --> 00:52:38,060
So then it says I
want some peanuts.

1095
00:52:38,060 --> 00:52:39,920
So then the low-level
policy scoops peanuts.

1096
00:52:39,920 --> 00:52:42,420
Then the high-level policy says
move the scoop into the bag.

1097
00:52:42,420 --> 00:52:44,045
The low-level policy
starts to do that.

1098
00:52:44,045 --> 00:52:46,212
At this point, you'll
actually notice that the robot

1099
00:52:46,212 --> 00:52:47,520
is about to make a mistake.

1100
00:52:47,520 --> 00:52:49,560
And so that's when
you can intervene.

1101
00:52:49,560 --> 00:52:52,890
And instead of telling the
robot move the scoop in the bag,

1102
00:52:52,890 --> 00:52:55,140
because it seems like the
low-level policy isn't going

1103
00:52:55,140 --> 00:53:01,600
to do that correctly, you
could then intervene and say,

1104
00:53:01,600 --> 00:53:02,840
move the scoop into the bag.

1105
00:53:02,840 --> 00:53:04,340
Or instead of saying
move the scoop,

1106
00:53:04,340 --> 00:53:07,020
you say move the
left arm to the left,

1107
00:53:07,020 --> 00:53:10,720
go higher, move the
scoop into the bag, then.

1108
00:53:10,720 --> 00:53:13,220
And so these user
commands are overriding

1109
00:53:13,220 --> 00:53:15,100
the high-level policy.

1110
00:53:15,100 --> 00:53:18,180
And then at this point,
you can give control back

1111
00:53:18,180 --> 00:53:19,600
to the high-level policy.

1112
00:53:19,600 --> 00:53:27,020
And the high-level policy
predicts, pour into the bag,

1113
00:53:27,020 --> 00:53:29,900
get some banana
chips, and so forth.

1114
00:53:29,900 --> 00:53:31,860
So that's what this sort
of DAgger intervention

1115
00:53:31,860 --> 00:53:34,340
looks in the high-level space.

1116
00:53:34,340 --> 00:53:36,500
And then after you fine-tune
the high-level policy

1117
00:53:36,500 --> 00:53:39,220
on those kinds of interventions,
the high-level policy

1118
00:53:39,220 --> 00:53:41,940
can then self-correct and
know that the low-level policy

1119
00:53:41,940 --> 00:53:43,780
is making a mistake,
where it was

1120
00:53:43,780 --> 00:53:46,100
about to make the same
sort of mistake again.

1121
00:53:46,100 --> 00:53:49,700
And then the high-level policy
says, go higher, and then

1122
00:53:49,700 --> 00:53:53,403
move the scoop into the bag to
prevent that sort of mistake.

1123
00:53:53,403 --> 00:53:56,070
And so this is an example of the
high-level and low-level policy

1124
00:53:56,070 --> 00:54:02,070
adapting together to
account for deficiencies.

1125
00:54:02,070 --> 00:54:03,870
Cool.

1126
00:54:03,870 --> 00:54:06,310
Now we've talked about
hierarchy versus flat policies.

1127
00:54:06,310 --> 00:54:06,910
You could ask.

1128
00:54:06,910 --> 00:54:08,950
You can actually
do a comparison.

1129
00:54:08,950 --> 00:54:12,710
In this particular
setup, does hierarchy

1130
00:54:12,710 --> 00:54:14,870
outperform using a
single flat policy

1131
00:54:14,870 --> 00:54:18,550
to do the long-horizon task?

1132
00:54:18,550 --> 00:54:21,130
This comparison is actually
from a different research paper,

1133
00:54:21,130 --> 00:54:25,110
but it has basically
the same approach.

1134
00:54:25,110 --> 00:54:26,830
In terms of task
progress, there's

1135
00:54:26,830 --> 00:54:29,070
a 34% improvement
from using hierarchy

1136
00:54:29,070 --> 00:54:31,110
versus training a
single policy to do

1137
00:54:31,110 --> 00:54:34,990
the full long-horizon
imitation learning task.

1138
00:54:34,990 --> 00:54:36,730
And in terms of
following instructions,

1139
00:54:36,730 --> 00:54:38,290
there's also a gap.

1140
00:54:38,290 --> 00:54:42,110
This is this instruction
accuracy metric.

1141
00:54:42,110 --> 00:54:44,390
And you can also
actually compare

1142
00:54:44,390 --> 00:54:48,825
DAgger to imitation learning
for the high-level policy.

1143
00:54:48,825 --> 00:54:50,200
And you can also
see that there's

1144
00:54:50,200 --> 00:54:52,200
an improvement from using
DAgger compared to just using

1145
00:54:52,200 --> 00:54:53,380
vanilla imitation learning.

1146
00:54:53,380 --> 00:54:54,880
And this is the
improvement that you

1147
00:54:54,880 --> 00:54:56,547
get from adopting the
policies together.

1148
00:54:56,547 --> 00:55:00,320

1149
00:55:00,320 --> 00:55:03,070
Cool.

1150
00:55:03,070 --> 00:55:04,500
The nice thing
about hierarchies,

1151
00:55:04,500 --> 00:55:05,940
you can do longer horizon tasks.

1152
00:55:05,940 --> 00:55:10,760
And so with this full
system, you can, for example,

1153
00:55:10,760 --> 00:55:14,320
get the robot to put three
objects into a Ziploc bag, which

1154
00:55:14,320 --> 00:55:16,560
is traditionally a
pretty difficult task

1155
00:55:16,560 --> 00:55:20,840
to do, especially in one go.

1156
00:55:20,840 --> 00:55:22,500
And likewise with
a similar system,

1157
00:55:22,500 --> 00:55:25,160
you can also get a robot to--

1158
00:55:25,160 --> 00:55:27,260
the high-level command
is clean the bedroom.

1159
00:55:27,260 --> 00:55:28,677
And then the
low-level policy will

1160
00:55:28,677 --> 00:55:31,660
output things like pick up
shirt, put shirt in basket,

1161
00:55:31,660 --> 00:55:34,640
pick up shirt, put
shirt in basket.

1162
00:55:34,640 --> 00:55:39,360
And later pick up the
pillow, adjust the blanket,

1163
00:55:39,360 --> 00:55:42,040
put the pillow at the top of
the bed, adjust the pillow,

1164
00:55:42,040 --> 00:55:45,517
pick up tissue, put the tissue
in the trash can, and so forth.

1165
00:55:45,517 --> 00:55:47,100
And so these are
things that are like,

1166
00:55:47,100 --> 00:55:52,228
would be very hard to do if you
just train a single flat policy.

1167
00:55:52,228 --> 00:55:54,020
So instead of using
language, we could also

1168
00:55:54,020 --> 00:55:57,788
use images as that
intermediate representation.

1169
00:55:57,788 --> 00:55:59,580
And so what this would
look like is instead

1170
00:55:59,580 --> 00:56:01,940
of having the high-level
policy output language,

1171
00:56:01,940 --> 00:56:03,878
you would output a goal image.

1172
00:56:03,878 --> 00:56:05,420
And so it'd be like,
this is the kind

1173
00:56:05,420 --> 00:56:08,907
of an image of what I want you
to accomplish in the future.

1174
00:56:08,907 --> 00:56:10,740
In practice, you can
use a generative model,

1175
00:56:10,740 --> 00:56:12,540
like an image-editing
model to do

1176
00:56:12,540 --> 00:56:15,060
this, that takes as input
the image of the current step

1177
00:56:15,060 --> 00:56:18,252
and then edits that to look like
an image at a future timestep

1178
00:56:18,252 --> 00:56:19,710
that you want the
robot to achieve.

1179
00:56:19,710 --> 00:56:22,220

1180
00:56:22,220 --> 00:56:24,100
And then the low-level
policy would no longer

1181
00:56:24,100 --> 00:56:25,940
be language conditioned
but would rather

1182
00:56:25,940 --> 00:56:28,460
be a goal image
conditioned policy.

1183
00:56:28,460 --> 00:56:31,940
And so you can think of this as
a kind of a multi-step inverse

1184
00:56:31,940 --> 00:56:35,060
model that takes as
input the current image,

1185
00:56:35,060 --> 00:56:37,300
some future image, and
then outputs the actions

1186
00:56:37,300 --> 00:56:41,020
that you can take to
get to that goal image.

1187
00:56:41,020 --> 00:56:43,660
And then both of these
will be trained again

1188
00:56:43,660 --> 00:56:45,620
with imitation learning.

1189
00:56:45,620 --> 00:56:47,870
And one of the things
that's pretty nice

1190
00:56:47,870 --> 00:56:50,790
is that you no longer need to
segment videos with language

1191
00:56:50,790 --> 00:56:52,590
annotations to do this.

1192
00:56:52,590 --> 00:56:55,870
You just need the videos
of the long-horizon task.

1193
00:56:55,870 --> 00:56:58,330
And to train this
high-level policy,

1194
00:56:58,330 --> 00:57:02,110
you can train it on robot
data and predict future images

1195
00:57:02,110 --> 00:57:04,910
in the trajectory, like
from 1 second or 5 seconds

1196
00:57:04,910 --> 00:57:08,057
into the future, or 10
seconds in the future.

1197
00:57:08,057 --> 00:57:10,390
But you could also actually
train this high-level policy

1198
00:57:10,390 --> 00:57:14,070
using YouTube videos,
or something like that,

1199
00:57:14,070 --> 00:57:16,830
to predict what are future
images that might accomplish

1200
00:57:16,830 --> 00:57:19,670
some long-term goal.

1201
00:57:19,670 --> 00:57:22,550
And that sort of
unlabeled video data

1202
00:57:22,550 --> 00:57:25,310
can be helpful, especially
if data from the robot

1203
00:57:25,310 --> 00:57:26,140
is more sparse.

1204
00:57:26,140 --> 00:57:29,830

1205
00:57:29,830 --> 00:57:31,990
So then an example of
what this looks like

1206
00:57:31,990 --> 00:57:36,150
is, say, the task is
to move the wooden bowl

1207
00:57:36,150 --> 00:57:37,630
to the top of the table.

1208
00:57:37,630 --> 00:57:40,150
You pass that into the
high-level policy that

1209
00:57:40,150 --> 00:57:44,110
then generates images, which
are shown with the pink border,

1210
00:57:44,110 --> 00:57:47,610
and then that generated subgoal
image with the pink border

1211
00:57:47,610 --> 00:57:50,130
is passed as input to the
low-level policy, which

1212
00:57:50,130 --> 00:57:51,750
then predicts actions.

1213
00:57:51,750 --> 00:57:53,250
And both of these
policies again are

1214
00:57:53,250 --> 00:57:57,410
trained with imitation
learning on demonstration data

1215
00:57:57,410 --> 00:58:00,090
and potentially also if
for the high-level policy

1216
00:58:00,090 --> 00:58:03,255
can be trained on
video data as well.

1217
00:58:03,255 --> 00:58:05,630
So this one is putting the
toothpaste in the wooden bowl.

1218
00:58:05,630 --> 00:58:07,880
You can see that it generates
these subgoal images of,

1219
00:58:07,880 --> 00:58:10,930
for example, holding the
toothpaste above the bowl.

1220
00:58:10,930 --> 00:58:12,190
It then reaches that.

1221
00:58:12,190 --> 00:58:15,310
Then it generates a subgoal
of lowering the arm.

1222
00:58:15,310 --> 00:58:20,732
And so the robot lowers the
arm to complete the task.

1223
00:58:20,732 --> 00:58:23,190
And then this last example,
the goal is to open the drawer.

1224
00:58:23,190 --> 00:58:26,970
The first subgoal is to put
the gripper on the handle.

1225
00:58:26,970 --> 00:58:30,890
Then the next subgoal is to
move the gripper to the right

1226
00:58:30,890 --> 00:58:34,362
so that it opens, and so forth.

1227
00:58:34,362 --> 00:58:36,570
Another thing that's nice
about this sort of approach

1228
00:58:36,570 --> 00:58:41,602
is that if your
policy sees objects

1229
00:58:41,602 --> 00:58:43,810
that it's never seen before
that are hard to describe

1230
00:58:43,810 --> 00:58:46,460
in language, then you--

1231
00:58:46,460 --> 00:58:49,180
obviously, that's something
that it can more easily describe

1232
00:58:49,180 --> 00:58:52,180
in images because it can
more easily generalize

1233
00:58:52,180 --> 00:58:53,700
across the objects
that it's seeing

1234
00:58:53,700 --> 00:58:55,600
and generate pixels
for those images,

1235
00:58:55,600 --> 00:58:58,060
if it doesn't know what the
semantic name for that object

1236
00:58:58,060 --> 00:58:59,780
is.

1237
00:58:59,780 --> 00:59:00,920
Cool.

1238
00:59:00,920 --> 00:59:02,420
And then for this
sort of thing, you

1239
00:59:02,420 --> 00:59:05,006
can also do a
comparison on like,

1240
00:59:05,006 --> 00:59:08,660
is it helpful to include those
unlabeled videos for training

1241
00:59:08,660 --> 00:59:10,740
the high-level policy?

1242
00:59:10,740 --> 00:59:12,620
And if you only
use robot data, you

1243
00:59:12,620 --> 00:59:14,340
get performance that
looks like this.

1244
00:59:14,340 --> 00:59:16,780
And if you add videos
of humans for training

1245
00:59:16,780 --> 00:59:21,140
the high-level policy, you
get an increase in performance

1246
00:59:21,140 --> 00:59:25,940
compared to just using the
robot data to train the policy.

1247
00:59:25,940 --> 00:59:30,300
Now, we talked earlier
about how chain of thought

1248
00:59:30,300 --> 00:59:31,560
can replace hierarchy.

1249
00:59:31,560 --> 00:59:33,860
And there's also approaches
that have developed

1250
00:59:33,860 --> 00:59:36,260
chain of thoughts versions
of this, where you can have

1251
00:59:36,260 --> 00:59:38,720
some high-level task and
the current observation,

1252
00:59:38,720 --> 00:59:42,293
and then actually output either
subgoal images or subtasks.

1253
00:59:42,293 --> 00:59:43,960
You can also have
other representations,

1254
00:59:43,960 --> 00:59:47,200
like bounding boxes
or waypoints, and then

1255
00:59:47,200 --> 00:59:48,520
output the action.

1256
00:59:48,520 --> 00:59:51,960
And there are some
recent approaches

1257
00:59:51,960 --> 00:59:56,480
from the last one year,
basically, quite recent,

1258
00:59:56,480 --> 00:59:59,760
that do something like that.

1259
00:59:59,760 --> 01:00:03,520
The other thing to note is that
this is with imitation learning.

1260
01:00:03,520 --> 01:00:05,040
And we'll talk
about reinforcement

1261
01:00:05,040 --> 01:00:06,160
learning in a minute.

1262
01:00:06,160 --> 01:00:09,920
But generally for these
large-scale systems to date,

1263
01:00:09,920 --> 01:00:12,640
for the most part, they use
imitation learning more so

1264
01:00:12,640 --> 01:00:13,960
than reinforcement learning.

1265
01:00:13,960 --> 01:00:17,600
And fine-tuning them with
reinforcement learning

1266
01:00:17,600 --> 01:00:20,920
is, in some ways, an
open research question

1267
01:00:20,920 --> 01:00:23,520
for like, what is the best
recipe for accomplishing that

1268
01:00:23,520 --> 01:00:24,280
at large scales?

1269
01:00:24,280 --> 01:00:26,600

1270
01:00:26,600 --> 01:00:28,600
It's also a pretty important
research direction,

1271
01:00:28,600 --> 01:00:31,573
because if we think about
this slide right here,

1272
01:00:31,573 --> 01:00:33,240
the kind of performance
of these systems

1273
01:00:33,240 --> 01:00:36,260
is still not super,
super reliable.

1274
01:00:36,260 --> 01:00:38,760
I should mention, these are in
pretty challenging situations

1275
01:00:38,760 --> 01:00:42,140
with held-out objects that
the robot hasn't seen before.

1276
01:00:42,140 --> 01:00:44,090
So it's kind of out of
distribution compared

1277
01:00:44,090 --> 01:00:45,810
to what it saw during training.

1278
01:00:45,810 --> 01:00:48,130
But if we had a way to train
these with reinforcement

1279
01:00:48,130 --> 01:00:49,755
learning, then we
should be able to get

1280
01:00:49,755 --> 01:00:52,170
these performances to
be much higher compared

1281
01:00:52,170 --> 01:00:54,770
to using imitation learning.

1282
01:00:54,770 --> 01:00:57,050
So the question is, like we
talked about personalized

1283
01:00:57,050 --> 01:01:01,050
recommendation systems, could
hierarchy be useful for that?

1284
01:01:01,050 --> 01:01:05,530

1285
01:01:05,530 --> 01:01:08,150
It's not something
that I've seen before.

1286
01:01:08,150 --> 01:01:10,610
And generally when
I've seen hierarchy,

1287
01:01:10,610 --> 01:01:13,850
it's usually been for pretty
longer-horizon problems

1288
01:01:13,850 --> 01:01:16,090
and tasks that are
more complicated.

1289
01:01:16,090 --> 01:01:18,010
In language modeling,
it's been used

1290
01:01:18,010 --> 01:01:24,330
for math problems,
essentially, and things

1291
01:01:24,330 --> 01:01:28,710
where you need to have multiple
steps for completing something.

1292
01:01:28,710 --> 01:01:29,210
Yeah.

1293
01:01:29,210 --> 01:01:31,070
I could think about it.

1294
01:01:31,070 --> 01:01:31,570
Yeah.

1295
01:01:31,570 --> 01:01:34,370
So in this case, the
high-level policy

1296
01:01:34,370 --> 01:01:37,190
is an image generative model.

1297
01:01:37,190 --> 01:01:40,890
And what it does is it's
going to take as input.

1298
01:01:40,890 --> 01:01:44,590
It's trained to take
as input, like an image

1299
01:01:44,590 --> 01:01:50,950
observation and some high-level
goal, like goal high level.

1300
01:01:50,950 --> 01:01:55,150
And then you have
a neural network.

1301
01:01:55,150 --> 01:01:57,590
And that neural
network is going to be

1302
01:01:57,590 --> 01:02:02,318
trained to output Ot plus k.

1303
01:02:02,318 --> 01:02:04,110
And so it's going to
be trained to generate

1304
01:02:04,110 --> 01:02:08,670
an image of a future image.

1305
01:02:08,670 --> 01:02:10,910
Yeah, trained to
generate a future image.

1306
01:02:10,910 --> 01:02:13,570
And you can get this
as if you have videos.

1307
01:02:13,570 --> 01:02:18,350
Then you don't need language
annotations to get this image.

1308
01:02:18,350 --> 01:02:21,550
In terms of the architecture,
it's using a diffusion model

1309
01:02:21,550 --> 01:02:22,610
to generate these images.

1310
01:02:22,610 --> 01:02:25,350
That's why you saw
the noise moving

1311
01:02:25,350 --> 01:02:29,090
into the image in the
direction of the goal.

1312
01:02:29,090 --> 01:02:29,590
Yeah.

1313
01:02:29,590 --> 01:02:34,270
So this particular goal might
be on the horizon of like--

1314
01:02:34,270 --> 01:02:35,770
I don't know-- a minute.

1315
01:02:35,770 --> 01:02:38,387
And this subgoal could
be in the direction,

1316
01:02:38,387 --> 01:02:39,470
or it could just be like--

1317
01:02:39,470 --> 01:02:42,740
I don't know-- 10 seconds in the
future or something like that.

1318
01:02:42,740 --> 01:02:43,240
Yeah.

1319
01:02:43,240 --> 01:02:45,505
So in terms of using
image-editing models,

1320
01:02:45,505 --> 01:02:46,880
that general sort
of pre-training

1321
01:02:46,880 --> 01:02:48,797
can be useful because
it teaches the model how

1322
01:02:48,797 --> 01:02:50,480
to generate images, in general.

1323
01:02:50,480 --> 01:02:52,422
And then the videos
that are used,

1324
01:02:52,422 --> 01:02:53,880
there are actually
videos of people

1325
01:02:53,880 --> 01:02:55,900
doing different
tasks that have--

1326
01:02:55,900 --> 01:02:58,240
like a single-language
annotation for what the person

1327
01:02:58,240 --> 01:03:00,280
is doing in the whole video.

1328
01:03:00,280 --> 01:03:03,040
And so those videos
are especially

1329
01:03:03,040 --> 01:03:05,400
useful to include because
they're pretty relevant.

1330
01:03:05,400 --> 01:03:09,000
If instead you included
random videos on YouTube,

1331
01:03:09,000 --> 01:03:10,840
it's a little bit harder.

1332
01:03:10,840 --> 01:03:12,692
But it might still
teach it about--

1333
01:03:12,692 --> 01:03:15,240
if there's some
future thing, what

1334
01:03:15,240 --> 01:03:17,600
are images that could
happen in the future?

1335
01:03:17,600 --> 01:03:21,020
And that is useful pre-training
for this kind of task.

1336
01:03:21,020 --> 01:03:21,520
Yeah.

1337
01:03:21,520 --> 01:03:25,080
So you're asking like, what if
the language isn't accurate?

1338
01:03:25,080 --> 01:03:30,120
This is like a sketch
instead of a real image?

1339
01:03:30,120 --> 01:03:33,360
So good question.

1340
01:03:33,360 --> 01:03:36,720
I guess, first, if you
wanted to use sketches here,

1341
01:03:36,720 --> 01:03:39,250
you just want to ideally
train the low-level policy

1342
01:03:39,250 --> 01:03:40,792
to be able to use
that sort of thing.

1343
01:03:40,792 --> 01:03:42,167
And there are
actually approaches

1344
01:03:42,167 --> 01:03:44,810
that have taken images, and you
can convert them into a sketch

1345
01:03:44,810 --> 01:03:47,610
and train a low-level
policy to do that.

1346
01:03:47,610 --> 01:03:49,810
And you don't actually
necessarily need

1347
01:03:49,810 --> 01:03:51,490
this to be a full image.

1348
01:03:51,490 --> 01:03:53,290
These are just kind
of, I should mention,

1349
01:03:53,290 --> 01:03:54,828
two example representations.

1350
01:03:54,828 --> 01:03:56,370
And language is
actually particularly

1351
01:03:56,370 --> 01:03:58,917
popular as an intermediate
representation.

1352
01:03:58,917 --> 01:04:01,250
But there's lots of other
representations you could use,

1353
01:04:01,250 --> 01:04:04,108
including sketches, including--

1354
01:04:04,108 --> 01:04:06,650
I don't know-- like a trace of
where you want the robot to go

1355
01:04:06,650 --> 01:04:09,330
or something like that.

1356
01:04:09,330 --> 01:04:12,690
And then in cases where the
language isn't accurate,

1357
01:04:12,690 --> 01:04:14,970
if it is describing
something that's different,

1358
01:04:14,970 --> 01:04:18,770
then oftentimes the robot will
then follow that different task

1359
01:04:18,770 --> 01:04:22,610
until it's been told to
do something different.

1360
01:04:22,610 --> 01:04:25,990
If the high-level policy is
predicting language that is bad,

1361
01:04:25,990 --> 01:04:29,450
then the system will suffer.

1362
01:04:29,450 --> 01:04:33,370
There are some cases where the
low-level policy might also

1363
01:04:33,370 --> 01:04:34,640
ignore the high-level policy.

1364
01:04:34,640 --> 01:04:38,170

1365
01:04:38,170 --> 01:04:41,550
If you give it
gibberish, it might just

1366
01:04:41,550 --> 01:04:44,862
have some sort of, in some
ways, kind of default behavior

1367
01:04:44,862 --> 01:04:46,570
because it was trained
to output actions.

1368
01:04:46,570 --> 01:04:50,670

1369
01:04:50,670 --> 01:04:55,110
For different
states, for example,

1370
01:04:55,110 --> 01:05:00,630
if it's like holding
the pasta right here,

1371
01:05:00,630 --> 01:05:02,365
regardless of any sort
of language input,

1372
01:05:02,365 --> 01:05:04,490
chances are the next action
is actually to pour it.

1373
01:05:04,490 --> 01:05:06,090
And so if you don't give
it a language instruction,

1374
01:05:06,090 --> 01:05:08,170
or if you give it a noisy
language instruction,

1375
01:05:08,170 --> 01:05:09,710
it might still
continue to pour it

1376
01:05:09,710 --> 01:05:11,610
just because it knows
that for that state,

1377
01:05:11,610 --> 01:05:14,150
typically, that's
what you do next.

1378
01:05:14,150 --> 01:05:16,670
Now let's talk briefly about
hierarchical reinforcement

1379
01:05:16,670 --> 01:05:17,250
learning.

1380
01:05:17,250 --> 01:05:19,617
So when I was bringing
up examples of this,

1381
01:05:19,617 --> 01:05:21,950
I think that the hierarchical
imitation learning systems

1382
01:05:21,950 --> 01:05:28,710
are quite recent, and I
don't have too many, I think,

1383
01:05:28,710 --> 01:05:30,310
exciting examples,
or examples that I

1384
01:05:30,310 --> 01:05:32,950
was excited about with regard
to hierarchical reinforcement

1385
01:05:32,950 --> 01:05:35,050
learning.

1386
01:05:35,050 --> 01:05:37,500
There are a few example
systems that you can look at,

1387
01:05:37,500 --> 01:05:39,880
and I think that generally
getting these kinds of systems

1388
01:05:39,880 --> 01:05:41,800
to work really well,
potentially on top

1389
01:05:41,800 --> 01:05:44,558
of the initialized hierarchical
imitation learning systems--

1390
01:05:44,558 --> 01:05:46,600
something that would be,
I think, really exciting

1391
01:05:46,600 --> 01:05:48,440
and important to explore.

1392
01:05:48,440 --> 01:05:51,778
But generally, in terms of what
has been done and what exists,

1393
01:05:51,778 --> 01:05:53,320
you can do hierarchical
reinforcement

1394
01:05:53,320 --> 01:05:57,200
learning with trying to
reach different states.

1395
01:05:57,200 --> 01:05:59,120
And this could be the
relative target position

1396
01:05:59,120 --> 01:06:01,880
of some agents or some objects.

1397
01:06:01,880 --> 01:06:07,000
And one cool work
that does this is it

1398
01:06:07,000 --> 01:06:11,160
has this four-legged ant
agent that you might have

1399
01:06:11,160 --> 01:06:13,840
worked with in homework one.

1400
01:06:13,840 --> 01:06:16,263
And its goal is
to get over here.

1401
01:06:16,263 --> 01:06:17,680
And to get over
there, it actually

1402
01:06:17,680 --> 01:06:20,200
needs to push this
object to the right

1403
01:06:20,200 --> 01:06:23,840
and then move there
as a result. And then

1404
01:06:23,840 --> 01:06:26,380
there's also this example,
where it needs to get over here.

1405
01:06:26,380 --> 01:06:27,755
If it tries to go
directly there,

1406
01:06:27,755 --> 01:06:29,680
it will fall in this crevasse.

1407
01:06:29,680 --> 01:06:32,160
And instead, it needs
to push and make

1408
01:06:32,160 --> 01:06:36,940
a little bridge for
itself and walk over here.

1409
01:06:36,940 --> 01:06:39,562
And these are pretty
long-horizon problems,

1410
01:06:39,562 --> 01:06:41,520
where if you just try to
take actions greedily,

1411
01:06:41,520 --> 01:06:44,060
you won't be able
to get to the goal.

1412
01:06:44,060 --> 01:06:47,900
And so the work trained
a goal-conditioned policy

1413
01:06:47,900 --> 01:06:50,820
conditioned on goal
states with a reward

1414
01:06:50,820 --> 01:06:52,340
of reaching those goal states.

1415
01:06:52,340 --> 01:06:55,580
And then it trained a high-level
policy to output goal states.

1416
01:06:55,580 --> 01:06:58,932
And those goal states are
the high-level actions.

1417
01:06:58,932 --> 01:07:01,500
But it actually also
used hindsight relabeling

1418
01:07:01,500 --> 01:07:07,700
for the high-level
policy to actually

1419
01:07:07,700 --> 01:07:11,473
relabel high-level actions,
or to relabel the goals

1420
01:07:11,473 --> 01:07:13,140
that the high-level
policy should output

1421
01:07:13,140 --> 01:07:14,940
in certain circumstances.

1422
01:07:14,940 --> 01:07:17,700
And it used an
off-policy algorithm

1423
01:07:17,700 --> 01:07:20,800
to train this in order to
allow it to work effectively.

1424
01:07:20,800 --> 01:07:24,580

1425
01:07:24,580 --> 01:07:27,620
And then another work that
does hierarchical reinforcement

1426
01:07:27,620 --> 01:07:29,360
learning uses language goals.

1427
01:07:29,360 --> 01:07:30,640
So you could use goal states.

1428
01:07:30,640 --> 01:07:33,420
You could also do
language goals.

1429
01:07:33,420 --> 01:07:35,850
This trains a
language-conditioned policy.

1430
01:07:35,850 --> 01:07:38,870
It uses language relabeling.

1431
01:07:38,870 --> 01:07:40,630
So instead of goal
relabeling, just

1432
01:07:40,630 --> 01:07:42,950
hindsight relabeling
in language space.

1433
01:07:42,950 --> 01:07:44,350
And then the
high-level policy is

1434
01:07:44,350 --> 01:07:47,150
trained to output
language, which is actually

1435
01:07:47,150 --> 01:07:48,360
a pretty difficult task.

1436
01:07:48,360 --> 01:07:51,270

1437
01:07:51,270 --> 01:07:53,330
Again, this is a work
that's not too recent.

1438
01:07:53,330 --> 01:07:55,510
And so it's from 2019.

1439
01:07:55,510 --> 01:07:57,910
And so the tasks are
relatively simple,

1440
01:07:57,910 --> 01:08:00,748
where it's trying to be able to
arrange these different objects

1441
01:08:00,748 --> 01:08:03,290
in particular order, where you
need to push the green object,

1442
01:08:03,290 --> 01:08:05,530
push the red object, the
blue object, and so forth.

1443
01:08:05,530 --> 01:08:08,710

1444
01:08:08,710 --> 01:08:09,465
Cool.

1445
01:08:09,465 --> 01:08:11,590
And then the last thing
that I'll mention is that--

1446
01:08:11,590 --> 01:08:13,298
we've talked about
these scenarios, where

1447
01:08:13,298 --> 01:08:16,229
you're supervising the low-level
policy to reach some goals

1448
01:08:16,229 --> 01:08:18,790
and the high-level policy
to output those goals.

1449
01:08:18,790 --> 01:08:24,569
Now, there's, in some ways, a
really cool research direction

1450
01:08:24,569 --> 01:08:26,990
of-- instead of
actually supervising

1451
01:08:26,990 --> 01:08:31,710
it to do certain goals,
can you actually discover

1452
01:08:31,710 --> 01:08:34,290
subtasks or discover
skills that might

1453
01:08:34,290 --> 01:08:37,490
be useful without
requiring supervision

1454
01:08:37,490 --> 01:08:40,609
on those low-level skills?

1455
01:08:40,609 --> 01:08:45,090
And here's one example of a work
that studies that, where it--

1456
01:08:45,090 --> 01:08:47,170
in this kind of simple
half-cheetah agent

1457
01:08:47,170 --> 01:08:49,689
that you might have also seen
in one of your homeworks.

1458
01:08:49,689 --> 01:08:52,609
It's able to learn skills
like backing up, walking

1459
01:08:52,609 --> 01:08:58,050
forward, doing I think a
front flip, and so forth.

1460
01:08:58,050 --> 01:09:00,231
And then in principle,
if you discovered skills

1461
01:09:00,231 --> 01:09:01,689
without supervision,
then you could

1462
01:09:01,689 --> 01:09:05,370
try to sequence those
together to do harder tasks.

1463
01:09:05,370 --> 01:09:07,529
But this is generally still
an active research area

1464
01:09:07,529 --> 01:09:10,430
and something that's pretty
challenging to scale up,

1465
01:09:10,430 --> 01:09:14,729
especially in very
large state spaces.

1466
01:09:14,729 --> 01:09:15,930
Cool.

1467
01:09:15,930 --> 01:09:16,645
That's it.

1468
01:09:16,645 --> 01:09:18,770
And next time, we'll be
talking about reinforcement

1469
01:09:18,770 --> 01:09:20,228
learning for
robotics, specifically

1470
01:09:20,228 --> 01:09:23,569
learning with
autonomy, and a guest

1471
01:09:23,569 --> 01:09:27,720
lecture from Ashish Kumar
on the Tesla Optimus team.

1472
01:09:27,720 --> 01:09:32,000

